window.harnessChunks["rows-cbd64866d7fb819a.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T19:44:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lavinhoque33/omp-deck\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OmpDeck</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lavinhoque33/omp-deck/tree/792cbff3d8c2ab041e03dcc33c5583fba38b4694\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 792cbff3d8c2ab041e03dcc33c5583fba38b4694\">792cbff &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform model-directed work across coding sessions and produce workspace outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Work needs different tools, project scopes or parallel investigations depending on intermediate results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next action, including whether to create, prompt, wait for or steer operational sessions through the installed tool surface. - Decision owner: The configured model inside the required omp operation cell. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OmpDeck&#39;s bundled extension, gateway dispatch, child handles and persistent terminal host. - Closure path: Workspace evidence and first-party child tool results return to the same or originating model session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model determines what to work on next and selects platform operations. The host supplies lifecycle, routing and tool enforcement. - Boundary reachability: The shipped extension registers OmpDeck operations in supported live-control sessions. The native gateway starts and governs the corresponding omp processes when the operator enables those tools. - Evidence: Required session runtime · Registered tool choices · Native tool dispatch and closure - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: The coding brain is a separately installed dependency. The claim credits its wired participation, without importing its unrelated standalone capabilities.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Partition parallel coding runs to reduce checkout and branch interference. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two workers launched against the same repository can otherwise edit the same checkout or change its branch state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the fan-out task partition and use or change the supplied per-child worktree arrangement. - Decision owner: Originating model using the enabled fan-out tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Gateway fan-out state, default worktree isolation, Git branch support, and combined result delivery. - Closure path: Worker prompts execute in distinct worktrees. Returned handles and results let the originating model adjust subsequent work or select a finish path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The caller model chooses whether and how to partition the work and may select branches. The runtime creates and tracks the chosen isolation. - Boundary reachability: The bundled extension registers fan-out and the gateway invokes the group execution path with worktrees enabled by default. - Distinct S1 units: Parallel child omp sessions each execute an initial task in its own persistent process and can choose task actions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Parallel writes and branch operations would alter each other&#39;s working tree when those sessions share the same checkout. - Attenuating coordination relation: The model selects a multi-task fan-out with separate Git worktrees and branch options; the gateway binds each child before its prompt runs. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Children operate from the selected paths and the opener obtains structured outcomes and can send a follow-up or initiate finish. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Separate mutable Git trees prevent a specific shared-checkout conflict. Session messaging alone supplies no such attenuation. - Evidence: Fan-out tool and defaults · Group lifecycle and returned result · Worktree creation - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Regulate current commitments and process use across the installation&#39;s active fleet. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Work can remain active while detached and retain memory or continue an unneeded commitment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose which independently running sessions to stop or restart from the shared current state. - Decision owner: Installation operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace running dock, session status and resource view, process controls, and optional idle parking. - Closure path: Chosen process stops or restarts while the workstation retains other sessions and their work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Default idle parking enforces a configured policy. A model can manage owned child work but no equivalent installed fleet-wide mandate was established. - Boundary reachability: The packaged live fleet view exposes every tracked running session and permits targeted stop from the session manager or backend. - Whole-system current view: Running dock and session manager enumerate open and detached running sessions, with state and process-tree memory. - Current-control decision scope: Operator can retire selected current process commitments independently of mere tab visibility and keep the other sessions running. - Evidence: Running views, controls and scope · Fleet sensing · Scoped model intervention - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Challenge a session&#39;s change report using the actual Git changes and feed human findings back to coding work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A worktree may contain unintended edits, incomplete changes or discrepancies that the session transcript does not expose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The reviewer chooses which actual diff findings to return to the originating session. - Decision owner: Independent human reviewer. The shipped assessment-specific path constructs access and corrective return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Changes panel, raw Git changes, review request and path highlighting, line-anchored prompt builder, gateway reply. - Closure path: Reviewer comments return through the waiting request or are submitted to the originating omp session for revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent can request review but cannot supply the independent reviewer judgment through its own completion summary. - Boundary reachability: The shipped extension exposes requestreview, which opens the gateway review path and waits for a reviewer response from the author-independent changes pane. - Claim being audited: Whether the session&#39;s reported repository changes correspond to acceptable worktree changes. - Ordinary reporting path: The prompted coding session reports in its transcript and status or completion reply. - Complementary access path: The Changes panel reads Git worktree and index diffs and gives the reviewer independent line-level visibility. - Independence boundary: The reviewer inspects Git data read by the host and composes the finding separately from the session narrative. - Who acts on findings: The named coding session receives the review comments and can act on them in a corrective turn. - Evidence: Review request and response · Direct Git evidence · Line-anchored feedback · Operator workflow - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Human review is optional and a dismissed or empty review produces no corrective finding. C credits the supplied complementary evidence and return construction.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Adopt a newly released installation capability while preserving access to active sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: External releases can improve or alter the desktop and gateway capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose whether to apply an eligible surfaced OmpDeck update. - Decision owner: Installation operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Release fetch and comparison, matching asset, verification, update and reopen path. - Closure path: The installed app restarts on the selected release and reattaches saved tabs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The release monitor discovers options; the user decides adoption. - Boundary reachability: Supported packaged installs expose the shipped update check and operator-triggered update and restart. - External distinction: Externally published release metadata and assets provide the candidate version. - Future / prospective distinction: The replacement will change subsequent operation while existing sessions can be reattached. - Adaptation option generated: Detect a newer eligible version for the observed installation type. - Path back into current capability / S3: The selected update replaces the installed desktop binary and restarts into the upgraded operating path. - Evidence: Update workflow · Release comparison and application - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Close an installation-level identity or ultimate-policy decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The operator can enable different agent tools, choose providers and set scoped workspace policies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No path resolving an identity or ultimate-policy matter and returning it to operation was established. - Decision owner: The operator chooses scoped tool exposure, permissions and settings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Opt-in tool roster, approval categories, project conventions, settings and agent definitions. - Closure path: Future session tools operate under the selected configuration and scoped approval. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool grants and workspace policies constrain ordinary operations. No credited owner resolves S3–S4 policy tensions or installation identity. - Evidence: Scoped tool enablement · Session authority dispatch · Settings and user modes - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4b4a531bed7ded29e4f63e7b3003e2da9cd036a5/assessments/om/ompdeck.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4b4a531bed7ded29e4f63e7b3003e2da9cd036a5\">4b4a531 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:10:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xingjian-bai/opal\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OPAL</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xingjian-bai/opal/tree/e999c21ee1a05891ea09d7d29bf2cd6597ca686b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e999c21ee1a05891ea09d7d29bf2cd6597ca686b\">e999c21 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out the training-optimizer task through model-chosen executable updater/analysis behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Loss/gradient dynamics, parameter roles, code failures and the operator&#39;s fixed training goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Keep or edit updater/analysis programs and choose the corresponding next optimization behavior. - Decision owner: The model-backed AgentWorker decision actor in supported live backends. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persistent backend session, decision workspace, syntax/closure checks, program loader, update-boundary installation, DDP transport, trial snapshots and rollback. - Closure path: Agent edits are collected from the workspace, checked, installed as active programs and executed on subsequent updates; actual outcomes/errors and adoption status are exported into later decisions. - Boundary reachability: wrap instantiates the first-party worker/backend and supplies its prompt/workspace; apply and install make completed choices active. The repository owns the observation-to-decision-to-training organization even though a coding CLI supplies the actor runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves the unchanged starting optimizer or keep backend and deterministic safeguards; the discretionary program choices do not occur. - Evidence: opal/wrapper.py; opal/agent.py; opal/backends/init.py; opal/backends/claude.py; opal/programs.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection establishes the shipped path, not successful live provider execution or product effectiveness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separate S2 inter-operational-unit stabilization organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential rank disagreement and stale asynchronous program answers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right and closed feedback path established. - Decision owner: No first-party function-specific decisive owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DDP synchronization, agree/checkranks, one worker in flight and file-level base merge. - Closure path: The inspected paths do not return a decision closing this metasystem function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ranks/parameter groups are replicas or parts of one logical training operation. Version agreement and stale-answer merge preserve one optimizer&#39;s consistency; they do not establish a regulator of interference among distinct autonomous S1 units. - Evidence: opal/wrapper.py; opal/agent.py; opal/misc/ddp.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection establishes the shipped path, not successful live provider execution or product effectiveness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately established whole-system current-control function at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Decision spending, waiting time, current training constraints and task failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right and closed feedback path established. - Decision owner: The operator selects fixed constraints; the agent owns bounded optimizer decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: budgetleft/costreached, schedule/waiting policy, watchdog, program adoption and recovery. - Closure path: The inspected paths do not return a decision closing this metasystem function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses local optimizer behavior; the wrapper enforces host-selected budget, horizon and safety rules. No whole-system organizational resource/commitment bargaining or intervention mandate is supplied. Parameter-wise learning rates remain task algorithm choices, not S3 merely because they allocate step sizes. - Evidence: opal/wrapper.py; opal/schedule.py; opal/misc/waiting.py; opal/backends/base.py; opal/prompts/neutral.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection establishes the shipped path, not successful live provider execution or product effectiveness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Provide complementary evidence challenging whether evaluation measurements reflect lasting training progress rather than temporary parameter substitution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An updater can move weights for a validation measurement and restore them afterward, misleading ordinary reported performance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Detect/report suspicious parameter round trips and enable an independently mandated reviewer/controller to judge their significance and corrective action. - Decision owner: The shipped Integrity sampler/analyzer deterministically owns diagnostic flags; an autonomous investigating audit actor and corrective mandate still require composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Seeded tensor samples/ring buffers, validation-opened windows, versioned evalintegrity events, stderr warnings and HTML report banner. - Closure path: Flags reach operator-facing events/report and direct the operator to inspect programs before trusting metrics. Automatic intervention and an autonomous review-to-control return are not shipped; this is the remaining constructor obligation. - Boundary reachability: With validation=N, evalintegrity defaults on in the real wrapper. reportvalidation opens a window and per-update recording resolves it; report.integrityflag consumes the resulting events. This is runtime code rather than only a test fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The source supplies an audit-specific independent reality-access and diagnostic/report path, rather than a generic log hook. It does not supply agent-owned audit judgment or correction, so C rather than A is credited. - Evidence: opal/misc/integrity.py; opal/wrapper.py; opal/report.py; tests/testevalintegrity.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded constructor claim. - Caveats: The agent is not told of the check, but generated Python is not proven unable to evade it. Flags do not stop/revert training; warnings alone are not completed autonomous audit closure. - Claim being audited: That the observed validation performance describes the durably trained model produced by the active updater. - Ordinary reporting path: Generated analysis reports and scalar validation measurements delivered to the optimization worker. - Complementary access path: Wrapper-owned sampling of actual parameter entries before/after externally reported validation, independent of agent-written analysis.py. - Independence boundary: The sampler and flag/report code are outside the generated programs and hidden from the agent prompt; testtheagentisnottold corroborates the documented separation. This is bounded access separation, not a security proof. - Who acts on findings: The operator is prompted to inspect saved programs. An application must compose an independent audit actor/authority and returned corrective control to close autonomous enactment.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Develop and adopt prospective optimizer/observation capability changes from the training environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing loss/gradient behavior, validation-versus-training divergence, performance cost and the remaining future update horizon. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Interpret the observed trajectory, form a predicted adaptation option, keep or rewrite the active optimizer/analysis, and revise that option after its consequences. - Decision owner: The persistent model-backed decision worker owns the bounded adaptation choice; the operator fixes the larger training identity and constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Training observations/inbox, versioned programs, syntax/build checks, apply/install, trial/fallback machinery and prior-outcome messages. - Closure path: The prompt requires predicted effect and falsification timing; AgentWorker passes current evidence and prior outcomes to the model. Completed option files become the active updater/analysis at a boundary. Their subsequent training effects/reversions return in the next request and prediction comparison. - Boundary reachability: This loop is wired by wrap/AgentWorker in the standard live backend mode; submit exports losses/reports/horizon, apply/install execute adopted files, and formatmessage/previousanswer report consequences. No user-written adaptation controller or adjacent research agent is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime can enforce a cap or reject code, but cannot select the prospective algorithmic response without the model. The same actor may perform S1 work and S4 adaptation; implementation co-location does not erase the distinct environment-to-capability role. - Evidence: opal/prompts/neutral.md; opal/agent.py; opal/wrapper.py; opal/programs.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at the declared optimizer-run boundary. - Caveats: This is within-run adaptation of deployed optimizer capability. Merely writing code or updating memory would not suffice; the credited evidence includes explicit future prediction, executable adoption and subsequent environmental feedback. No empirical improvement or cross-task transfer is established. - External distinction: Actual training-workload loss/gradient/validation and latency distinctions arrive from outside the optimizer decision cell; the operator-fixed model/data/loss are its environment. - Future / prospective distinction: totalupdates and remaining decisions constrain the horizon; the prompt requires a predicted effect, by when, and what would falsify it, then comparison with observed outcomes. - Adaptation option generated: A kept or revised updater.py/analysis.py (and helper modules), changing the live update rule and/or the observation capability. - Path back into current capability / S3: wrapper.apply checks/merges the option and install replaces the active runtime objects; later updates use them and submit returns their consequences. This is a capability-return path, without claiming a separately agent-owned S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity/policy adjudication path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Proposals to change training purpose, data/model/loss or permitted optimizer scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right and closed feedback path established. - Decision owner: Operator/developer own the initial training purpose and constraints; no first-party runtime S5 organization is supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static prompt/spec, backend workspace policy, code diagnostics and precision contracts. - Closure path: The inspected paths do not return a decision closing this metasystem function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator defines the goal and immutable training boundaries in advance. Ordinary notes, cost approval and code boundary rules do not provide an identity-level issue referral, legitimate authoritative resolution and returned governance loop. - Evidence: opal/prompts/neutral.md; opal/backends/claude.py; opal/misc/boundary.py; opal/wrapper.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection establishes the shipped path, not successful live provider execution or product effectiveness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/52222052384204425c189f7f913fcd1cbad74bbf/assessments/op/opal.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 52222052384204425c189f7f913fcd1cbad74bbf\">5222205 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T18:36:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/supernovae/SwarmOS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SwarmOS</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/supernovae/SwarmOS/tree/c85010787b001981beb84e60502c1eaca3c6f812\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c85010787b001981beb84e60502c1eaca3c6f812\">c850107 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously interpret approved task acceptance criteria and produce/revise code, plans, tests and evidence for an owned WorkUnit. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unpredictable repository content, technical uncertainty, tool results, compile/test failures and defects within the approved task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive plan, implementation, and corrective tool/code steps in response to observed workspace/test feedback. - Decision owner: the configured managed model-driven planner/developer/tester agent in a first-party launched role, not the Git queue or lease manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role prompts, task bundle, native CLI adapters, isolated worktree, checkpoints, approved-base policy, claim fencing and report import. - Closure path: issue contract and approved base → launched role receives source/task context → model-driven work and tool actions → tests/environment feedback → changed artifact and implementation handoff or repair. - Boundary reachability: internal/workflow invokes configured role runners for approved WorkUnits in the shipped swarm issue work path, with native Codex and other qualified adapters; no repository-development-only actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: after removing the model-driven worker, CAS/validation/PR services still enforce custody but cannot choose the task-specific implementation; only supported managed workers supply that discretion. - Evidence: pinned docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, docs/WORKFLOWS.md, internal/workflow/executeimplementation.go, internal/workflow/executevalidation.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for reachability and role ownership, not for success on every provider/runtime combination. - Caveats: individual provider model/tool loops are external dependencies; their unrelated S2–S5 mechanisms are excluded. Some platform/provider qualification is explicitly pending.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference between separate workers claiming or publishing the same issue state and return the conflict result to later worker behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous worker claims, stale renewal/checkpoint publication, and one worker overwriting another&#39;s accepted state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept exactly one current claim under a fencing token, reject an old claimant, and cancel or prevent publication from the superseded attempt. - Decision owner: constructor-defined CAS/lease policy executed by SwarmOS rather than an autonomous coordination agent choosing its own exception policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Git compare-and-swap refs, claimid, TTL, heartbeat, joint issue/checkpoint update, worktree isolation. - Closure path: rival claims or changed Git state → one fenced CAS succeeds and the other fails → stale worker is canceled/refused and must reacquire or hand off before subsequent work/publishing. - Boundary reachability: the claim/store implementation is mandatory for managed issue attempts in supported local/GitHub ledger modes, and subsequent artifact publication revalidates its claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the inter-worker conflict is real and transactionally closed, but the decisive resolution rule is prewritten, so coordination is constructor-controlled rather than an S2 agent discretion. - Distinct S1 units: separate concurrent managed coding-agent worker attempts/instances assigned against a shared issue/repository work organization. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate claim/overwrite or a stale worker publishing a checkpoint after another acquired the issue. - Attenuating coordination relation: unique fenced owner and CAS replacement rule, with cancellation/refusal on lease loss. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: rejected/stale worker cannot continue or publish; subsequent work must use the current claim/checkpoint or be stopped. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the code explicitly prevents a concrete clash of otherwise independent operational workers and returns the prevention to their later execution capability, not merely storing a task list. - Evidence: pinned docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, internal/coordination/store.go, internal/coordination/revoke.go, internal/workflow/executerun.go. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a narrow collision-attenuation path, not proof of a broad adaptive peer-negotiation metasystem.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate present task commitments, release/stop conditions and required verification for the approved WorkUnit across planner, developer, tester and publication stages. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unapproved plan changes, stale workers, overrun repair loops, untrusted candidate changes to validation policy, and reported completion before required checks pass. - Decisive decision or feedback right: withhold implementation or submit readiness when approved digests/checks/claim evidence disagree, reserve bounded repairs, and permit handoff/continuation only after policy-conforming current state. - Decision owner: constructor policy and authorized human WorkUnit/gate settings; managed agent roles produce artifacts but do not override approval, claim, or baseline validation predicates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkUnit digest and approved base SHA, phase machine, lease manager, protected-path checker, trusted-base validation specification and repair attempt budget. - Closure path: whole-issue current phase/artifact/claim/check results → deterministic admit, pause, retry, reject or finish transition → subsequent worker role and PR stage follows that decision. - Boundary reachability: internal/workflow/executerun.go, executevalidation.go and internal/pr/service.go govern every standard managed issue execution and publication path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the present-work control function is real but its decisive stage/authority rules are authored and enforced by SwarmOS policy; a model cannot autonomously waive them. - Whole-system current view: approved work objective, policy/base digests, lease/owner, phase, reports, checkpoint, required-check outcomes and repair budget for one WorkUnit. - Current-control decision scope: work admission, stage continuation, checkpoint trust, bounded retry, and readiness for an explicit submission; not a universal multi-project portfolio budget. - Evidence: pinned internal/contract/types.go, internal/workflow/executerun.go, internal/workflow/executevalidation.go, internal/pr/service.go, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for a bounded issue-level S3 constructor path. - Caveats: most approval and policy choices are parent or developer-owned; S3=C does not promote static enforcement to autonomous S3=A.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge an implementer/plan author&#39;s own report against independent source/acceptance evidence and return findings into corrective work before continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: self-reported correctness, incomplete acceptance coverage or a plausible implementation passing only its author&#39;s own checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately invoked reviewer/tester model assesses an exact plan/candidate against independent artifact/source/check evidence and produces change requests or failure findings. - Decision owner: the configured distinct reviewer/tester model-driven role; deterministic parsing, validation enforcement, and report import do not own the model&#39;s challenge judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: snapshot-isolated read-only review, output-only REVIEW.md, separate VALIDATION.md, trusted-base checks, role/provenance separation, bounded repair routing. - Closure path: author plan or candidate + self-report → separate reviewer/tester inspects source/acceptance and records findings → verdict/failing check blocks continuation → developer corrects candidate and the relevant check repeats. - Boundary reachability: the shipped agent review gate runs a configured reviewer in an isolated snapshot, while the standard validation phase invokes a separate tester and routes failed acceptance/check outcomes into bounded developer repair. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an independently invoked role receives the actual artifact and makes the substantive challenge, whereas SwarmOS&#39;s gate merely enforces its outcome. The positive claim is limited to modes with a distinct configured reviewer/tester. - Claim being audited: that a proposed plan or implemented candidate satisfies the approved scope/acceptance and can safely progress. - Ordinary reporting path: planner&#39;s plan/test plan or developer&#39;s implementation report and claimed commands/results. - Complementary access path: read-only isolated review of source/plan and separate tester&#39;s direct acceptance/required-check execution rather than only trusting the prior handoff. - Independence boundary: reviewer is isolated from modification of the author&#39;s worktree, and the tester is a separately invoked role with acceptance checks; the model&#39;s actual provider can be configured, so independence is actor/process and evidence-path, not automatically different model vendors. - Who acts on findings: SwarmOS stops promotion and returns failed validation or requested changes to the developer/plan path for bounded correction and recheck. - Evidence: pinned internal/agent/review.go, internal/workflow/executereview.go, internal/workflow/executevalidation.go, docs/WORKFLOWS.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; independence is deployment/role-configuration-sensitive. - Caveats: static required checks alone are routine validation, not the S3 witness. Human-only review is not counted as autonomous S3; the cited distinct model-driven reviewer/tester mode is required.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped first-party future-capability adaptation loop over the SwarmOS work organization was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: issue backlog changes, new requests and tool/runtime upgrades are operational inputs, but not a demonstrated environment-model → prospective adaptation-option → changed capability loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users author new WorkUnits, repository maintainers choose product changes, and current-work agents choose implementation within a given contract. - Decision owner: external user/maintainer for future capability and present S1 roles for bounded tasks; no separately closed SwarmOS S4 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: issue triage, backlog scan, role guidance, diagnostic reports, roadmap and release process. - Closure path: incoming issue or planned product change enters an ordinary approved WorkUnit; no first-party S4 actor is shown returning a prospective environment-derived option into durable operating capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sensing a new issue, repairing a failing current PR and storing lessons are not by themselves outside-and-then capability adaptation. - Evidence: pinned docs/WORKFLOWS.md, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, ROADMAP.md, internal/workflow/backloglanes.go. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a parent organization using SwarmOS could run an S4 practice outside this distribution; it is not imported here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no internally or parent-closed ultimate organizational identity/policy decision loop is established for a SwarmOS installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task constraints, protected paths, authentication and repository identity are set from outside as fixed policy/permissions, not deliberated by a first-party ultimate-policy authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: maintainer authorizes a task/plan and repository policy, but no shipped runtime path adjudicates a constitutional identity question and returns that ruling to govern the organization. - Decision owner: external repository maintainer/provider authorization for configuration and approvals; ordinary task actors operate inside those constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: immutable approval bindings, permission checks, WorkUnit policy, human gate, sandbox rules and versioned base SHA. - Closure path: configured policy is enforced for work; no identity/policy dispute → legitimate S5 adjudication → returned authoritative new identity/ultimate-policy state is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human approvals and static permission rules are concrete authority constraints, but they do not close S5 by themselves. - Evidence: pinned internal/contract/types.go, docs/project.md, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, internal/lease/. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a larger OpenSiro- or company-like parent organization may possess S5 externally; no such authority is credited to the shipped SwarmOS installation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/12dcd070e444e2d0246446e5e65421f725c294d6/assessments/sw/swarmos.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 12dcd070e444e2d0246446e5e65421f725c294d6\">12dcd07 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T00:17:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/quqxui/RSI-Flow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RSI-Flow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/quqxui/RSI-Flow/tree/0f87c2b198f22e5697853cfc6d0bf2d002e48ca7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0f87c2b198f22e5697853cfc6d0bf2d002e48ca7\">0f87c2b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A configured Pi/DeepSeek agent performs the substantive target task inside a first-party versioned workflow session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task instructions, tool/workspace state, model observations and failed subtasks under an operator-defined experiment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model chooses its response/action within an exposed task turn; results and tool feedback inform subsequent turns where the selected SDK supports them. - Decision owner: The model-backed target agent operating through the native driver; the engine and experiment runner do not choose its per-task response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Native SDK bridge, workspace/runtime snapshots, bounded prompt interface, execution timeout, credentials, receipts and result parsing. - Closure path: Input task and versioned target artifact → native model-backed agent turn → task/tool result → next agent turn and final response/experiment feedback. - Boundary reachability: Native integration is shipped under integrations/native/, wired to Pi/DeepSeek adapters and documented as supported task execution, rather than only a test mock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides task behavior; removing the native model leaves an experiment runner capable of testing artifacts, not autonomous task judgment. - Evidence: native worker, Pi driver, DeepSeek driver, README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Task-level S1 depends on the configured native agent/provider; built-in offline demonstration is not proof of model autonomy or real-provider success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party anti-interference coordination among distinct task-performing S1 cells is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent candidate trials could contend for resources or contaminate comparisons. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The framework bounds parallel work and separates trial artifacts; no inter-S1 conflict-specific adjustment is evidenced. - Decision owner: Preselected execution protocol and deterministic trial harness rather than an autonomous coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task batching, trial directories, stable task IDs, budget reservations and timeouts. - Closure path: Runner results are gathered into comparison; no disturbance-detecting cross-agent coordination decision returns to alter their working relationships. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Isolation and scheduling protect tests but are not the S2-specific regulation of interactions between ongoing autonomous S1 units. - Evidence: validation stage, budget ledger, native runner. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Different target frameworks may implement their own coordination; those internals are outside this review.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No agent-owned or specifically parent-governed whole-system discretionary control of concurrent current S1 priorities and commitments is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Budget exhaustion, failed trials, protocol violations, incomplete receipts and target-version conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Acceptance thresholds and stop/rollback predicates are fixed by operator-selected protocol; these do not choose new organizational priorities in a current whole-system view. - Decision owner: Human configuration supplies constraints, deterministic engine enforces them; no in-run S3 discretion is identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BudgetLedger, SingleRoundSearch, decide, deploy, pause/resume/cancel/recover and process isolation. - Closure path: Status and budget checks deterministically stop, reject or deploy a version; the engine does not revise commitments among distinct ongoing S1 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An operational gate and rollback authority do not become managerial judgment merely because they have effect on execution. - Evidence: engine, selection, adapters. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A custom SearchPolicy may implement additional control but no such standard autonomous whole-system owner is wired.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit of S1 claims with an audit-owned corrective return is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False task success, inaccurate version-loaded claims and unverifiable experiments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Evaluator, outcome receipts and load verifier check protocol predicates but do not exercise separate complementary organizational audit judgment. - Decision owner: The same experiment engine owns ordinary comparison/admission; no independently authorized auditor is composed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Paired evaluation, task receipts, exact-version digest, load verification, report evidence and regression thresholds. - Closure path: Comparison flows into ordinary Stage 6 acceptance; failed load checks trigger procedural rollback, not an independent audit finding sent to a corrective S3 owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Test comparison and defensive proof of load are valuable controls, but routine QA is not necessarily an independent complementary S3 challenge. - Evidence: validation, selection, reports. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: An operator can independently inspect raw receipts, but that is not an evidenced first-party audit-return mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: A configured reasoning agent generates prospective changes to the target agent&#39;s prompt/workflow from accumulated experience and diagnosed limitations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Recurring task failures, observed regressions, changes in future task mix and limits of the deployed artifact. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose candidate artifact changes, hypothesis and expected benefit, or explicitly propose no change; a subsequent protocol governs acceptance. - Decision owner: The configured model-backed CommandProposer (including the Codex integration) chooses a future adaptation option; the human-proposal mode separately supplies offline input but does not erase the supported autonomous path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Structured diagnosis, proposal context, key allowlist, versioned artifact build, baseline/candidate comparison, decide and verified deploy. - Closure path: Historical experience → diagnosis → reasoning backend chooses prospective artifact modifications → constrained build and paired evaluation → accepted new deployed artifact changes later target-agent sessions. - Boundary reachability: Engine.proposer selects command reasoning from config and Stage 4 invokes proposer.propose; the returned candidate flows into supported Stage 5 and Stage 6 deployment, not merely into documentation. - External distinction: Imported version-labelled task outcomes and feedback characterize target performance beyond the current agent turn. - Future / prospective distinction: Proposed prompt/workflow modifications concern future agent capability rather than a response to a present task. - Adaptation option generated: A structured candidate with hypothesis, expectedbenefit and allowed artifact changes, or nochange. - Path back into current capability / S3: The accepted content-addressed version is loaded/verified for subsequent task runs, with rollback after unsuccessful deployment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses the substantive candidate hypothesis and changes; deterministic gates can reject it but do not invent the adaptation option. - Evidence: proposal reasoning, engine proposer wiring, improvement, deployment. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Autonomous adaptation requires configured reasoning backend and provider; a candidate is not deployed without human-selected test thresholds and successful tests. Source evidence is not a live improvement-outcome claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party identity/ultimate-policy dispute resolution and binding return for the RSI installation is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: What targets, prompts, models, budgets and artifact fields should be legitimately allowed to change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human operator fixes target schema, allowed keys, model configuration and evaluation rules at initialization; no identity-policy issue is referred to an adjudicator for resolution. - Decision owner: Operator/developer outside the live run; model proposer is restricted to editable lower-level artifact fields. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable config hash, key allowlist, model identity snapshots, validation errors and manual stop. - Closure path: Fixed policy blocks out-of-bounds proposals; it does not carry an identity question to a legitimate policy owner and feed a fresh ruling back into operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Artifact edits and accepted versions adjust capability, not ultimate purpose/constitutional identity; immutable settings are enforcement not adjudication. - Evidence: definition, improvement, engine. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A developer may deliberately install new policies in a new workspace, but that is not a first-party governed S5 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/35a76015978b6753215beac6e12cb62e542ba822/assessments/rs/rsi-flow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 35a76015978b6753215beac6e12cb62e542ba822\">35a7601 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T19:32:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kumizeod/AgentForeman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentForeman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kumizeod/AgentForeman/tree/634f9c0906f133fc577d6291c29b8f8cfd6af02a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 634f9c0906f133fc577d6291c29b8f8cfd6af02a\">634f9c0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce candidate code/experiments and general task artifacts through model decisions with returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous goals, failed code/training, poor candidates and contradictory evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose experiment/repair/retest/stop and instructions; author source or artifacts; revise after independent criticism. - Decision owner: configured model-backed manager, coding worker, contributors and integrator, invoked by first-party protocols. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source snapshots, typed responses, worker pool leases, edit allowlist, native training/checks, archives and round/deadline bounds. - Closure path: verified baseline → manager model choice → worker action → independent measurement/criticism → next choice or revision → preserved terminal result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model roles leaves deterministic transport and checks but no substantive hypothesis, edit or report decision. - Caveats: A is structural ownership, not proof of live model quality; external coding tools are credited only as explicitly assembled task actors. - Boundary reachability: CLI campaign/overnight and cooperate/debate commands assemble these roles and execute their chosen actions; they are shipped supported modes, not test-only prompts. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/manager.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/engine.py; src/agentforeman/cooperate.py; src/agentforeman/debate.py; src/agentforeman/poolworker.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately closed inter-S1 interference regulation at the declared task boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent contributions can be inconsistent; worker contention and duplicate execution are mechanically bounded. - Decisive decision or feedback right: integrator chooses combined output, but contributors are not subsequently coordinated through a specific inter-unit correction loop. - Decision owner: integrator owns S1 synthesis; runtime owns fixed sequencing/leases, with no separately evidenced autonomous S2 discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: independent assignments, integration contract, queue/lease locks, quota cooldown and critique of combined artifacts. - Closure path: contributors return once → integrator revises the combined product; duplicate jobs are suppressed by fixed IDs/locks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: decomposition, waiting for all contributions and rewriting one combined answer do not establish mutual adjustment of operational units. - Caveats: does not deny coordination in a broader organization assembled around this tool. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/cooperate.py; src/agentforeman/pool.py; docs/COOPERATIVE.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system discretionary current-control function over a portfolio established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate failure, incumbent comparison, quota waits and bounded task duration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: manager chooses the next experiment within one fixed objective; it cannot change resources, task goal or budgets. - Decision owner: model owns local S1 research choices; operator/configuration selects task scope, role pool and limits; runtime enforces those choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: campaign rounds/best record, budget/deadline checks, role registry, UI status, cancel and resume. - Closure path: measured rounds return to a next-task-action choice; generic stop/confirmation returns no evidenced whole-system resource bargain. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the campaign manager name does not turn its local experimental policy into a metasystem; fixed quotas are enforcement. - Caveats: broader multi-project allocation may be performed externally but is outside the declared first-party campaign mode. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/manager.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/briefing.py; src/agentforeman/pool.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge operational claims through separate model audit/critique with complementary original evidence and corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported synthesis claims, missed criticism, inadequate experiment plans and false candidate success. - Decisive decision or feedback right: distinct critic/auditor chooses anchored findings and accept/revise/reject; campaign reviewer can block or return an executable proposal for revision. - Decision owner: separately invoked model reviewer/auditor roles; deterministic gates apply verdict consistency and run trusted checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct role identities, read-only review snapshot, source/check/artifact anchors, issue ledger, frozen verifier and bounded revision/retest. - Closure path: raw artifacts/source/check receipts → separate critic judgment → integrator/manager revision and recheck, or stopped/needs-evidence/rejected result; council audit conditions final disposition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model audit judgment owns substantive criticism; deterministic parsing and trusted checkpoint measurement are support, not the owner of that judgment. - Caveats: distinct identities are not statistically independent models; supplied sources are not authenticated by quote matching, and live factual correctness remains unmeasured. - Boundary reachability: documented campaign --review, cooperative distinct-reviewer mode and council audit are shipped runtime modes; no developer fixture is needed to select them. - Claim being audited: candidate/proposal or chair/integrator output is supported by original source material, controls, actual artifacts and check results. - Ordinary reporting path: manager rationale, proposer artifacts and chair/integrator summary. - Complementary access path: reviewer receives copied source snapshots plus experiment evidence; general critics receive full artifact/source/check anchor documents, and council auditor sees original answers and all criticism rather than chair summary alone. - Independence boundary: separate read-only sessions/role identities from author/integrator/chair; no authorship permission for the reviewer. Trusted verifier directly reads tensors without importing candidate code, adding corroboration rather than autonomous judgment. - Who acts on findings: campaign manager/integrator revises under recorded issue IDs; controller blocks/returns or marks needsevidence/rejected instead of fabricating success. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/discussion.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/debate.py; src/agentforeman/council.py; src/agentforeman/templates/moons/verify.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective adaptation loop changing AgentForeman operational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new research hypotheses, failed experiment results, provider quota changes and user task uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: manager may change candidate experiment code, not harness capability/organization; task chair clarifies current requirements. - Decision owner: task agents own research outcomes; maintainer/operator owns external product evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: research plans, measurements, council sources, quota information and roadmap. - Closure path: research findings return to the current task; no prospective environmental judgment returns as a change to harness capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research as the primary operational transformation is S1; internal optimization and quota reaction do not establish S4. - Caveats: an operator could use outputs to redesign its organization, but that external act is not a shipped adaptation closure. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/manager.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/council.py; src/agentforeman/briefing.py; docs/ROADMAP.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop established beyond bounded task contracts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unclear scope, changed deliverables/acceptance/autonomy and stale user confirmation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human confirms a task contract; agents must pause when they report a scope change, rather than decide ultimate policy. - Decision owner: operator owns the local task mandate; product identity remains maintainer-defined outside runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: version/fingerprint confirmation, allowed-decision/pause fields, frozen checks, scope-change signal and stop controls. - Closure path: briefing → exact human confirmation → bound child task; requested scope changes pause for new clarification. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: this is operational task admission and authority bounding; it is not an identity/ultimate-policy dispute between present operations and future adaptation. - Caveats: contract editing is a real returned human decision but does not by itself satisfy the function-first S5 threshold. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/briefing.py; src/agentforeman/taskcontract.py; src/agentforeman/debate.py; docs/BRIEFING.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8100f851f4f20a52d8b11424bbccf95b1d296801/assessments/ag/agentforeman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8100f851f4f20a52d8b11424bbccf95b1d296801\">8100f85 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T20:13:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/3ae3ae/harness2-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness² CLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/3ae3ae/harness2-cli/tree/0c811344a4edfe34960b2fc8f286369d7e81fb27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0c811344a4edfe34960b2fc8f286369d7e81fb27\">0c81134 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Bounded model-backed improvement work per encountered task: examine operational evidence, edit a harness, probe the edited candidate and produce a selected task harness plus result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different user problems and actual coding-agent failures demand different instruction, skill, script or hook changes; candidate edits can also violate a harness component contract. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured improver chooses candidate file changes and explains them against evidence. The model selector (in automatic mode) chooses a final task result where multiple candidates exist. - Decision owner: Model-backed proposer/editor and optional automatic selector, invoked through the first-party session interface. External executor cognition is a dependency, but the improvement goal and agent-edit control path are shipped by the reviewed CLI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace harvest, component allowlist, tree validation, per-stage retry/fallback, rollout adapter, result/trajectory records and candidate selection parser. - Closure path: Current base-task rollout → independent agent-edited harness trees → validated candidate task probes → composed candidate and selector result → accepted task-harness and evidence for later reuse. - Boundary reachability: cli.run constructs Proposer with the installed channel config and invokes schedule.runsweep; runtask calls recursion.runmode, whose runstage actually invokes Proposer.call with writable context/harness and captures its changed tree, then probes candidates. All are included in the shipped CLI mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model makes contentful harness edits and, when configured, evidence-driven task-result choices; source-local Python merely bounds and enforces those decisions. Dry-run, static tests and deterministic fixtures by themselves do not prove model autonomy. - Evidence: run.py, recursion.py, proposer.py, schedule.py. - Basis: Supported live-model session path at the frozen commit, not asserted empirical success rate. - Confidence: High for source-wired decision ownership; outcome quality untested. - Caveats: The target coding assistant and provider are externally configured and not extra first-party viable S1 units.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced S2-specific stabilization among two distinct, separately viable owned S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent candidate and domain execution might consume resources, but distinct S1 divisions and an inter-unit oscillation/collision control relation are not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic concurrency width, domain ordering, semaphore and run-label isolation coordinate experimental steps, not an agentic inter-S1 conflict response. - Decision owner: Static Python scheduler and operator configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ThreadPoolExecutor, proposer semaphore, namespace isolation, mode/domain libraries and run budget. - Closure path: Scheduled experiments complete and aggregate; no S2-specific sensed interference and returned reciprocal adjustment across autonomous operating units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Stage-level parallelism, composition and result pooling are parts of one bounded optimization process, not S2 because they run concurrently. - Evidence: schedule.py, recursion.py, proposer.py. - Basis: Scoped source negative after examining task and domain concurrency. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An external multi-agent product using this CLI might provide S2 independently.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-current control relationship over independently viable operational divisions at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Budget overruns, candidate failures, task completion, user approval and project file drift are local run/deployment constraints rather than multi-unit resource/commitment governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The operator sets execution budget, task set and allowed surface; runtime enforces limits; selectors compare results. None revises current commitments across distinct S1 units with systemic authority. - Decision owner: CLI user for configuration, deterministic scheduler for enforcement and model for candidate choice; no separately supported S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Budget/call caps, run status, concurrency limit, retries, apply conflict checks and --force option. - Closure path: Fixed run settings → bounded task experimentation → output; absent whole-current S3 view, discretionary priority/resource intervention and returned binding adjustment to multiple ongoing units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Picking a better harness is an operational or prospective adaptation choice; enforcing an operator&#39;s preset budget is not agent-owned S3 and ordinary apply approval is not parent-governed whole-system control. - Evidence: run.py, schedule.py, apply.py. - Basis: Bounded negative of whole-organization current-control closure. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Operator may manage a larger organization, but its current control is not implemented by this application.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently complementary audit channel challenges routine S1–S3 reports and closes findings into corrective systemic control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Bad candidate harness files, inaccurate agent result claims and poor task answers are checked by local tree validation, rollout comparisons, benchmark judging or optional evaluation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Ordinary candidate validators, task selector and eval compare outputs; none has separately independent authority to audit a standing S3 report and route corrective findings to management. - Decision owner: Deterministic validators/evaluation routines, configured model selector or human comparator within the same adaptation process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Harness component checks, anonymous candidate selection, result reports and user-triggered eval. - Closure path: Candidate/result evidence → within-loop selection and optional later quality measurement; no complementary audit → binding whole-current corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent improver proposals and blind selection reduce some bias, but are routine production/evaluation, not an independent S3 oversight path. - Evidence: selection.py, eval.py, judge.py. - Basis: Scoped negative distinguishing quality checks from complementary audit independence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A separate external QA team could supply such audit for a deployed agent but is outside this reviewed system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: Model-backed across-task prospective harness adaptation with a second, explicitly supported parent-controlled blind-selection mode that changes which candidate and lesson enter the future task library. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar project tasks, newly observed errors and weak transfer of task-specific improvements to later tasks; user may also disagree with automated candidate choice. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Automatic selector chooses the adopted candidate and reflector extracts a transferable lesson; in --selector human mode the user personally selects among blinded final candidates while a model reflector can distill the adopted result. The choice feeds subsequent tasks through the library. - Decision owner: A: model-backed selector and reflector/proposer in CLI sweep; P: operator at the shipped interactive selector, not the benchmark author or external research institute. Each mode has separately attributable adoption authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-domain sequential Library, selectionpool, anonymous candidate files, human TTY prompt/choice, adopted tree and experience persistence, context.build injection into later proposer contexts; final project deployment is optional with apply. - Closure path: Encounters and candidate execution evidence → automatic/human chosen task harness → model-reflected generalizable lesson → modelib.add → next task&#39;s proposal context includes earlier domain library → potential changed subsequent harness. After a run, generated consolidated harness may also be explicitly applied to project files. - Boundary reachability: Shipped cli/run.py chooses HumanSelector when selector.channel.kind == human; otherwise it instantiates model-backed Proposer. Both invoke schedule.runsweep and selection.runselection; schedule.runonemode calls reflectadoption and conditionally modelib.add, while next task&#39;s runstage loads the library. cli/apply.py separately permits reviewed real project-file adoption. - External distinction: Actual newly encountered user task domains and contrasted coding-agent trajectories expose operational shortcomings not specified in a fixed harness. - Future / prospective distinction: Per-domain transferable lessons plus consolidated project harness target future tasks, rather than merely grading or repairing one immediate deliverable. - Adaptation option generated: Model editor proposes changed skills/instructions/scripts/hooks; model/human selector adopts a tested candidate; reflector writes future-reuse cues; consolidation proposes project-wide next-task harness. - Path back into current capability / S3: Selected experience is persisted in the per-domain library and read by subsequent model proposer sessions, changing the options considered for later edits. The optional apply command installs consolidated or one-task adopted components into the user&#39;s project; no independent S3 manager is asserted. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A has agent-owned candidate choice and lesson generation with model decisions returning through first-party memory to later operations. The distinct P mode assigns the same consequential selection right to the user through a real terminal interaction and returns it through the same library. This does not assign the separately guarded final installation to the model. - Evidence: run.py, human.py, selection.py, schedule.py, library.py, apply.py. - Basis: The two first-party shipped prospective adaptation loops share the persistent future-facing library, but have distinct actual adoption decision owners. - Confidence: High for reachable branch/closure, medium for model generalization since no model runs were made. - Caveats: --selector human requires a TTY and multiple finalists (k &gt; 1); at k=1 selection is trivial. A failed reflection yields no transferable lesson. Project apply defaults to human approval and is not evidence of autonomous project deployment.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate ultimate identity/mission-policy adjudication is shipped for the overall improvement organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Operator task selection, allowed editable files, model/provider choice, benchmark scope and safety limits bound execution but do not create a separate ultimate policy decision loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User chooses and configures the run and can approve/force project-file writes; no identity-level conflict is escalated to a legitimate authority and returned as governing policy. - Decision owner: External developer/project owner for the overall purpose; shipped runtime for validation and configured model for bounded edits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI config and setup, component tree validator, source boundaries, apply confirmation and git-dirty checks. - Closure path: User-provided goal and constraints → local optimization and optional installation; absent system identity dispute → authority judgment → returned revised governing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Supervised application is an operational/capability adoption gate, not by itself an identity- or ethos-level S5 decision. The parent-mode S4 classification does not automatically imply S5=P. - Evidence: settings.py, run.py, apply.py. - Basis: Bounded negative for identity-level authority and operational return. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The user&#39;s broader software organization may have S5; this repository does not establish its governance authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b4570aa846bcb5c7e3c02ccabec88b26877d4335/assessments/ha/harness-cli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b4570aa846bcb5c7e3c02ccabec88b26877d4335\">b4570aa &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T18:56:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amosblomqvist/learnharness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LearnHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amosblomqvist/learnharness/tree/3dc88472eea41eed0d5e5f29c2f16f8d063c415b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3dc88472eea41eed0d5e5f29c2f16f8d063c415b\">3dc8847 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform goal-directed teaching, research, demonstration construction and visual/PDF assistance under a chosen lesson mission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain starting competence, unexpected wrong answers, unfamiliar facts, tool failures and domain-specific problems requiring outside evidence or visualization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Active prober/teacher models choose questions, explanation revisions, quiz checks, graph lookup and specialist delegation; specialist models choose searches, fetches, demo edits/tests and visual construction steps. - Decision owner: Shipped real Agent and runSubagent loops invoked through first-party models and tools; mere persistence/UI are not S1 decision owners. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-turn persisted reconstruction, probe-to-teach seam, schema-validated question/plan tools, model selection and BYOK gates, structured worker toolsets, source-specific job persistence and abort handling. - Closure path: Learner goal/response → prober or teacher model decides action → asks/teaches/checks, or dispatches a specialist model → learner answers or worker returns evidence/artifact → teacher uses new information in following action. - Boundary reachability: handleChatRequest constructs buildAgent for the prober/teacher and jobTools dispatches to real shipped subagent runners; explicit configured model keys are needed. Scripted test engines do not supply the credited autonomy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model agents select semantically different learning questions, explanation paths, search/fix iterations and specialist output; fixed question grading and JSON work queues alone cannot choose these. - Evidence: teacher runtime, job tool dispatch, researcher with web tools, demo builder with real headless check. - Basis: Shipped, model-backed adaptive operation and separately executable agent specialties. - Confidence: High source-path, medium live-provider efficacy (not executed). - Caveats: BYOK and required tool capabilities are deployment prerequisites. Tests use fake engines and do not validate pedagogical outcomes with real users.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Would attenuate a conflict/oscillation specifically between independent lesson S1 workers and feed a revised coordination commitment into those workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing background job completions, shared graph writes, multiple specialist reports or two S1 units reaching incompatible conclusions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Per-chat wake/steer deduplication and global graph audit lock serialize events; the teacher commissions specialists and receives their reports. No first-party autonomous inter-specialist disagreement negotiation or adjusted joint commitments is shown. - Decision owner: Deterministic job registry and semaphore control the mechanics; teacher handles the educational consequence as its S3/current-lesson decision, not a distinct inter-S1 S2 arbitration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-chat serialized wake delivery and recovery, durable pending result IDs, global limited concurrency, source-local extraction single-flight and global serialized audit/merge. - Closure path: Queued worker completion is delivered to teacher once; two graph audits cannot concurrently read and write a stale graph. This is mechanical admission/stability without a distinctive semantic response returned to the conflicting S1 workers. - Boundary reachability: The named locks, duplicate IDs, consent gates and worker result steers are live first-party source paths, but no separate model mediator resolves S1-vs-S1 work collision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Correct sequencing protects state, yet methodology requires specific inter-S1 disturbance, distinct discretionary response and returned adjustment of S1 behaviour, not delegation or a queue alone. - Evidence: serialized delivery / wake protocol, graph audit gate and source single-flight, six-system topology. - Basis: Bounded negative for agent-owned inter-S1 conflict attenuation; credible structural serialization is not enough. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A user can ask the teacher to reconcile contradictory reports; no dedicated shipped inter-S1 coordination mandate is thereby established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Keep one current learning session coherent across the learner&#39;s goal, assessed understanding, dependency plan, teaching steps and commissioned specialist work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Learner confusion invalidating current plan assumptions, wrong facts, delayed external support, mismatch between learning target and work in progress, specialist failures and changed user constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The teacher has the current session transcript and goal, authors/revises the visible plan, decides which knowledge gap matters now, allocates research/demo/visual/PDF work and changes explanation/check sequence based on returned worker results and learner answers. - Decision owner: Model-bearing first-party teacher for the session-local teaching mission; learner can change the overall request, but deterministic planinit/queue enforcement is not the author of a new plan or delegation decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Prober handoff, current session record, DAG validator, question node tags, job input and result steers, durable wake, one-turn active registry, disabled-tool and model-role settings. - Closure path: Prober establishes starting point → teacher builds global lesson dependency tree → learner&#39;s answers or independent job results reveal a current gap → teacher revises explanation/plan and initiates/cancels/uses supporting work → next quiz/lesson state reflects that decision. - Boundary reachability: buildAgent(&quot;teacher&quot;) is attached after the real probedone handoff, has plan and job tool access in the supported chat pipeline; background worker result messages return to this same persistent session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: It is more than a blind router: the teacher decides a session-wide pedagogical priority and changes ongoing commitment to the goal in response to actual feedback. It does not manage the entire multi-session application, unrelated practice sets or a global provider budget. - Whole-system current view: Session transcript, learner goal, active plan/progress, commissioned worker result steers, and optional read-only graph inspection relevant to the session. - Current-control decision scope: Revise dependency plan/teaching sequence, task requests to multiple agents and response to produced evidence within the declared one-session recursion. - Evidence: teacher planning and on-the-fly repair, tool registry and model loop, worker feedback into live teacher, job instruction and correction. - Basis: Source-closed current session-wide semantic decision/return using autonomous teacher. Not a claim of product-wide S3 at higher recursion. - Confidence: Medium; stronger for declared one-session recursion than a broader multi-session organization. - Caveats: Plan progress tags are deterministic, not a second autonomous controller. No source proof of reliable cognitive diagnosis is inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Reconcile the identity/novelty claim of graph propositions extracted from the session against separately read existing graph evidence before committing the update. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Duplicate concepts minted by distinct extraction runs or different sessions, same fact paraphrased as different graph nodes, or identity links based on stale graph snapshots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A distinct model auditor explores the existing graph through find/listsessions/listpropositions and submits links; mergeFindings uses those judgments under the audit lock. It cannot inspect or fact-check the raw teaching source. - Decision owner: Separate first-party auditor agent makes semantic identity judgments, with deterministic serialization and merge applying them. No independent raw-source verification or challenge to extractor factual truth is present, so classify a narrow complementary constructor C, not general-purpose closed independent S3 A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Schema-bounded auditor submit, graph exploration tools, process-wide FIFO audit/merge lock, deterministic ledger writes, coverage checkpoints, rollback-on-stage-failure and source provenance. - Closure path: Extractor proposes lesson propositions → isolated auditor sees proposed propositions plus independently queried existing graph → returns identity links → merger collapses duplicate concepts or adds new ones → teacher can subsequently query the updated graph; no verified correction of teaching claims is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Enabled graph extraction invokes runAuditor in the standard source-to-ledger pipeline and mergeFindings consumes its output; BYOK extractor/auditor keys are required. It is a mandatory same-path graph integrity check, not a standing out-of-band lesson review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The auditor selects identity links through model exploration rather than code-only deduplication, but its complementary scope is narrow and the raw-source evidence boundary excludes stronger autonomous verification claims. - Claim being audited: Extractor&#39;s implicit assertion that newly proposed graph propositions are unique rather than semantic duplicates of an existing concept. - Ordinary reporting path: Extractor&#39;s submitted proposition list and source-local digest, subsequently represented as new graph nodes. - Complementary access path: Separate graph-aware model agent gets the live existing graph through first-party find/list tools while the extractor is isolated from that graph&#39;s full cross-source state. - Independence boundary: Distinct agent role, prompt and tools; no access to original source text, same application and possible underlying provider model; must not be confused with epistemic truth-checking. - Who acts on findings: The deterministic merger applies links immediately; the resulting graph is read-only exposed to future teacher sessions and the learner. - Evidence: auditor mandate and source exclusion, independent graph exploration tools, graph merge/evidence ledger. - Basis: Active model-backed narrow identity-audit constructor with complementary graph access; no independently validated correctness of the input claims. - Confidence: Medium for S3 constructor, high for its explicit limitations. - Caveats: Headless demo-browser tests occur inside the producing S1 worker and are not independent S3. A routine verifier in the same ingest path should not be promoted to a comprehensive audit of the lesson; future independent same-ref review could reasonably bound this to — if graph identity verification is considered inseparable production validation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would scout changing external/future learning or agent capabilities and return options that alter the lesson organization&#39;s capability rather than just answer a current fact question. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future model/tool shifts, curriculum/environment changes and novel teaching methods outside the present learner request. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Researcher retrieves outside evidence only for teacher&#39;s current informational need; learner/operator selects provider, prompt override and enabled tools by settings. No standing future-oriented strategy process is shown. - Decision owner: Teacher for current accuracy and human for manually changed app settings, not a distinct autonomous S4 opportunity/threat agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Search/fetch researcher, durable cross-session knowledge graph, provider roles, prompt/settings UI and saved learner records. - Closure path: External research returns a cited brief into the current lesson and can correct facts, while persistent graph can inform later sessions; neither source path generates and adopts a prospective capability-change strategy. - Boundary reachability: Actual researcher and settings/graph surfaces exist; no future-option-to-S3 contract is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fetching recent facts and cumulative memory do not themselves create an outside-and-then intelligence function. - Evidence: researcher prompt and web tools, teacher research requirement, global model settings. - Basis: Bounded negative: external information serves the current session instead of strategic adaptation. - Confidence: High for reviewed standard path. - Caveats: A user may ask about future trends as a lesson topic; the topic alone does not make its executor S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would resolve an ultimate identity/purpose legitimacy conflict for the learning organization with binding authority and return a governing policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting fundamental educational goals or competing legitimacy claims rather than everyday disagreement about the next lesson step. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Learner chooses or changes the study goal and app configuration; teacher&#39;s instructions prescribe a method and ask the learner to approve the plan. No standing binding constitutional-purpose adjudication is wired. - Decision owner: Human learner sets the mission external to the autonomous lesson, teacher regulates subordinate pedagogical commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Probe/goal question, approval before teaching, role prompts, per-user provider and prompt settings. - Closure path: Learner can change the study target, and the next session turn adapts. This is ordinary task definition and plan review, not an adjudicated fundamental identity-policy dispute. - Boundary reachability: Goal elicitation and plan confirmation are active, but identity-level disagreement resolution and binding organizational policy are not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A mandatory teaching philosophy in a system prompt is static implementation policy, not runtime S5 authority. - Evidence: probe/teach split, teacher&#39;s plan confirmation, fixed teaching identity. - Basis: Bounded negative for first-party policy legitimacy closure. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A learner remains the legitimate authority to select its task, but task selection does not on its own justify S5=P at the present recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e0377deccfa4315a6ad15781c29c8fa36bea6903/assessments/le/learnharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e0377deccfa4315a6ad15781c29c8fa36bea6903\">e0377de &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-27T14:18:29Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stanGSA/ZhiMing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ZhiMing</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-27\">2026-11-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stanGSA/ZhiMing/tree/9e092aecf37f9d7b92cbb22a67594721ce86b57b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9e092aecf37f9d7b92cbb22a67594721ce86b57b\">9e092ae &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce a structured, validated consultation response, or return a concrete request for clarification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different consultation questions, incomplete input, signals that disagree, tool validation errors and feedback on drafts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select a domain and the tools or specialists to consult, then assemble, repair and submit a result. - Decision owner: The configured model-backed consultation agent. In multi mode each specialist also owns its bounded tool and report choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role-specific registries, deterministic calculators, evidence references, output validation, persistence and bounded runtime. - Closure path: Tool results become model observations. The agent submits a grounded reading, patches a rejected draft or requests facts, and a successful terminal tool stores and renders the result. - Boundary reachability: The checked-in CLI and browser runtime construct the first-party consultation loop and registries. The CLI exposes both bundled modes without requiring the user to author an agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses how to handle the request from evidence and can repair outcomes; domain calculations and validation are external to that judgment. - Evidence: cli.py, app.py, app.py, agents/orchestrator.py, common/tools.py - Basis: Pinned first-party source inspection. - Confidence: high - Caveats: The application is scoped to a structured consultation and calculation product. The offline demo uses a different decision path and is not evidence for model ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Coordinate separate domain specialists whose outputs must share one consistent requested interval and evidence state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel consultations may duplicate work or compute mutually incompatible timeframes and write competing values into shared evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The orchestrator selects and normalizes the inputs and consult set, then decides which specialist to retry, omit or wait for when a report is incompatible or missing input. - Decision owner: The shipped multi-mode orchestrator model, with deterministic safeguards for shared state mutation and resource interference. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Isolated child sessions, shared input conventions, same-system in-flight dedupe, bounded parallel work, and ordered conflict-aware commit. - Closure path: A differing timeframe rejects a consult or contribution before it reaches shared reading state. The orchestrator receives failed/needs-input/retained evidence, then changes subsequent calls, collects one clarification, or continues explicitly with compatible surviving reports. - Boundary reachability: The runtime registers the specific consultation tool in supported multi mode; its results are committed and returned automatically through the same agent loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The orchestrator decides the shared consultation treatment and reacts to the coordination outcome. The timeframe check and single writer enforce the relation. - Distinct S1 units: Separate model-driven Tarot, Western astrology, BaZi, name and dream specialist sessions, each with its own role, registry and structured report. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent calls can span incompatible intervals or repeat a same-system computation, corrupting shared evidence assumptions. - Attenuating coordination relation: Pass a common timeframe, reject duplicate/inconsistent work, and write isolated contributions in call order only when compatible. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The parent model sees the specific coordination failure, remaining valid report or missing-input status and changes what happens next. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The tool implements a dedicated cross-specialist compatibility check and report validity decision, and the orchestrator receives the result as input to subsequent specialist work. - Evidence: agents/orchestrator.py, agents/consult.py, agents/consult.py, agents/consult.py, agents/consult.py, harness/loop.py - Basis: Pinned first-party source inspection. - Confidence: high - Caveats: Only the supported multi-agent mode supplies this relation. The repository defaults to single mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct inside-and-now organizational control function was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent consultations can contend for one request&#39;s tokens and time. The web service also has session lifecycle and health reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No distinct function-specific decision or feedback right was established. - Decision owner: No distinct owner at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Hierarchical token holds and reserve, request timeout, consult semaphore, report status and optional server alert checks. - Closure path: A per-request child is admitted, waits, or fails within fixed limits and the agent chooses how to complete the individual case. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model manages one consultation rather than reallocating current responsibilities or resources across a broader operating portfolio. The deployed monitoring script emits observations and alerts. - Evidence: app.py, harness/budget.py, agents/consult.py, agents/orchestrator.py, ops/monitor.py - Basis: Pinned first-party source inspection. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary operational audit path was established inside the consultation harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A draft can omit disagreement, cite unsupported facts or otherwise fail output policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No distinct function-specific decision or feedback right was established. - Decision owner: No distinct owner at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: A separate reviewer role, deterministic source-reference validation, run traces, and an adjacent service monitor and knowledge-review pipeline. - Closure path: A revise verdict returns to the authoring loop for repair. The reviewer sees the assembled draft and the same signal projection, and may only submit its verdict. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer has a separate session and genuine task-level feedback. It does not independently acquire operational evidence for reviewing a manager&#39;s representation of current work. - Evidence: agents/reviewer.py, agents/reviewer.py, common/tools.py, knowledge/reviews/README.md, ops/monitor.py - Basis: Pinned first-party source inspection. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective capability adoption loop was established for the installed consultation runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User profile, time-sensitive case inputs and underlying reference material can change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No distinct function-specific decision or feedback right was established. - Decision owner: No distinct owner at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Profile versioning, current calendar references, saved readings and a separately built versioned knowledge base. - Closure path: Input/profile changes affect subsequent readings. Runtime consumes a published knowledge database. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime updates current inputs and supports recall. Knowledge review and build operate on a maintainer release surface, with no bundled prospective runtime decision that adopts an organizational capability change. - Evidence: app.py, app.py, common/tools.py, config.yaml, knowledge/reviews/README.md - Basis: Pinned first-party source inspection. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate policy or identity settlement loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Inputs can conflict with fixed scope and safety rules; a deployer can select a model or review strictness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No distinct function-specific decision or feedback right was established. - Decision owner: No distinct owner at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: System prompts, shipped safety preflight, optional semantic judge, role boundaries and config switches. - Closure path: The active turn observes the policies and can issue a restricted response, use the allowed route or stop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These control concrete request handling under a predefined purpose. No supplied authority decides an identity/policy conflict and returns a newly authoritative operating policy. - Evidence: agents/orchestrator.py, app.py, app.py, app.py, common/tools.py - Basis: Pinned first-party source inspection. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/69ea498c5abfe0f912603c4ed41c249737322820/assessments/zh/zhiming.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 69ea498c5abfe0f912603c4ed41c249737322820\">69ea498 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:18:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Moissanite-Labs/exo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">exo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Moissanite-Labs/exo/tree/e9ad895d2c59e998f0358b982a9b8a4ac0ad2aea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e9ad895d2c59e998f0358b982a9b8a4ac0ad2aea\">e9ad895 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: persistent coding/assistant work with discretionary tool use and delegated subtasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying tasks, repository state, tool/provider errors and interrupted sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose tools, edits, commands and follow-up steps from current observations. - Decision owner: the main or configured delegated agent in the first-party Loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CoreEngine composition, router, tool runner, plugins/approval desk, context tree and typed result persistence. - Closure path: tool results are appended to messages before the next model round; steering and errors update subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: compose.create builds the current core, registers the delegator, connects runtool and starts Loop; no legacy Hermes host is required for this claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model discretion leaves tool/transport/persistence mechanisms without task-level transformation decisions. - Evidence: src/exo/compose.py; src/exo/core/engine.py; src/exo/core/loop.py; src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider APIs/claude-cli can supply inference; their unrelated harness functions are not borrowed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no bounded first-party inter-S1 coordination function established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generic child concurrency and shared-log consistency do not establish a concrete inter-S1 interference/attenuation/feedback witness. The writer lock excludes competing cores of the same persistent agent, not mutually adjusted S1 work cells; peer messaging/delegation alone is insufficient. - Evidence: src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py; src/exo/core/agentlog.py; src/exo/peers.py; src/exo/core/engine.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-current organizational control decision loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task decomposition, narrow task intervention and constructor-selected limits do not independently establish whole-system resource/commitment regulation. The reviewed commands expose current state but no additional function-specific S3 decision/return path beyond ordinary operations was evidenced; no P inferred from a generic operator UI. - Evidence: src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py; src/exo/subagents.py; src/exo/core/engine.py; src/exo/modelspend/policy.py; src/exo/scheduler.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit loop established for current operational claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Trace and findings are first-party observability/self-report with deterministic rechecks; generic critic delegation lacks an audit-specific claim/access/corrective contract. Learning critique and held-out adaptation testing concern prospective proposals, not a separately closed operational audit. No S3 is inferred from audit/reviewer terminology. - Evidence: docs/TRACE.md; src/exo/core/engine.py; src/exo/core/findings.py; src/exo/core/tools/builtin/harness.py; src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py; src/exo/learn/review.py; src/exo/learn/climb.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: adapt future behavior to the external principal&#39;s recurring needs and corrected preferences through typed, evidence-linked options. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current behavior no longer matches principal corrections/rulings or generalizable class-of-task expectations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: derive a beyond-instance lesson and adopt an eligible future behavior rule; alternatively the principal explicitly accepts/rejects a proposed adaptation. - Decision owner: base mode: aux reviewer/critic supplies adaptive judgment and bounded automatic promotion closes eligible ordinary lessons. Parent mode: legitimate store operator/principal decides the concrete proposal. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: principal authorship filtering, evidence/session/weight constraints, guarded/conflict holds, proposal diffs, promotion ledger and refreshed memory nodes. - Closure path: eligible autonomous promotions or explicit proposalaccept write accepted nodes/skills and ledger state; exo-memory reads nodes fresh for subsequent turns. Guarded/skill paths do not silently bypass their gates. - Boundary reachability: compose attaches learn to the current engine; onturnend starts review for new principal signals; review calls constrained applypolicy. CLI exo proposals exposes show/accept/reject, and accepted nodes return through installed memory. Optional slow API/fixture export is not the basis of automatic A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model develops the prospective behavioral lesson from external principal feedback; deterministic policy constrains its acceptance. In the explicit review mode the principal owns adoption. Mere transcript retention, fact recall or a roadmap is not the credited S4 witness. - Evidence: src/exo/compose.py; src/exo/learn/learner.py; src/exo/learn/review.py; src/exo/learn/promote.py; src/exo/consolidate.py; docs/LEARNING.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: base claim is limited to enabled store-backed, eligible generalizable behavior lessons; facts stored without a prospective behavior implication are not S4. Skill/held-out nightly automatic scheduling is not claimed; no empirical effectiveness or transfer result.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: retain ultimate authority over the harness&#39;s public/irreversible/financial risk-policy boundaries at the principal recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runtime gate policy changes could relax reserved authority or alter ultimate allowed exposure/risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adopt or withhold a changed governing gate policy, rather than merely approve one action. - Decision owner: the authenticated live principal; agent/peer input cannot supply that credential. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: principal transport/authorship checks, pinned policy snapshot, changed-policy hashes, stricter interim enforcement and signed acceptance record. - Closure path: gate status exposes changed policy; /gate accept requires principal authority, records policyaccept and replaces the pinned policy used to govern later calls. - Boundary reachability: installed exo-gate command routes accept to acceptpolicies; the current composed core loads plugins and carries principal authority through the transport path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ultimate policy adoption remains with the principal; deterministic classification, hashes and individual exact-text approval are supporting controls, not autonomous S5. - Evidence: plugins/exo-gate/exogate/hook.py; src/exo/principal.py; src/exo/compose.py; docs/GATE.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: bounded to the legitimate principal&#39;s ultimate risk/delegation policy; no claim that every approval, SOUL.md edit or maintainer action is S5. Required-executor behavior is mode-specific. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: who may authorize public/irreversible/financial acts and whether changed reserved-risk policy becomes governing. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: principal transport authority checked by requireprincipal; no internal autonomous S5 mode established. - Return-to-operation path: changed policy status/hash → live principal accept → logged policyaccept → new pinned policy → subsequent tool decisions.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37735444207e5ea17cd166d25eaa1dde9259c22e/assessments/mo/moissanite-labs-exo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37735444207e5ea17cd166d25eaa1dde9259c22e\">3773544 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-14T00:21:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist-v3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI-Scientist-v3</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-14\">2026-11-14</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist-v3/tree/df8313889a004f8a44a472e19a833cd547bb828a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision df8313889a004f8a44a472e19a833cd547bb828a\">df83138 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Conduct an autonomous scientific experiment campaign that chooses and revises candidate research implementations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain hypothesis quality, changing experiment feedback, runtime errors, variable metrics and competing candidate designs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The research agent chooses ideas, modifications and further attempts after evaluating feedback within a task mandate. - Decision owner: First-party invoked scientific research agent; the experiment executor and scoring code are enforcement/evidence mechanisms rather than the owner of idea generation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Experiment runner, model tools, persisted campaign state, score/manifest files, worker processes, compute reservation and checkpoint/resume. - Closure path: A selected idea is executed; metrics and failures are returned to the campaign loop; the agent changes the next experiment or selects the candidate to write up. - Boundary reachability: Standard shipped campaign entry path invokes the idea-generation and experiment loop and persists outputs usable by later iterations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the agent retains execution and scoring but not adaptive scientific hypothesis selection or iterative source changes. - Evidence: campaignloop.py, ideageneration.py, mainentry.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: This is autonomy within a researcher-defined task. No live scientific performance or experiment replication was executed for this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Attenuate operational disturbance between distinct coequal autonomous research S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel workers may race over shared results, GPU resources or duplicate investigative effort; this is a potential concrete interference to check, not proof of an agent-owned coordination function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped S2-specific discretionary coordinator and returned inter-S1 corrective decision was established in the campaign boundary. - Decision owner: Deterministic worker scheduler, cycle lease and host build-lock code; no independently evidenced S2-specific agent owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Thread locks, worker leases, per-round results isolation, shared-memory files and advisory file lock. - Closure path: Scheduling and locking avoid runtime collisions but do not show an autonomous coordination decision that redirects mutually interfering S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A worker lease assigns execution slots without organizational discretion over relations between separate operations. - Evidence: parallelhelpers.py#L1619-L1754, runtimebuildcoordination.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Govern current whole-system research commitments and resource allocations on behalf of several operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: CPU/GPU scarcity, failed workers and competing experiment candidates create operational control demands. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supported autonomous or parent-governed whole-system current-control decision distinct from campaign/task execution and fixed resource enforcement is established. - Decision owner: Task configuration/researcher sets experiment budget; deterministic scheduler and pruning routines enforce it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worker count, max cycles, GPU pool selection, queue stop flags, score-based pruning and persisted campaign manifests. - Closure path: Runtime budgeting changes how a single campaign proceeds, but an organizational whole-system current-control loop over multiple obligations is not evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Internal experiment selection and fixed budget enforcement are S1 work or infrastructure, not proof of S3 authority. - Whole-system current view: No established S3 actor has a governed view of multiple independent ongoing research commitments beyond single-campaign scheduling. - Current-control decision scope: No established independent S3 intervention right; the inspected scheduler may stop or assign cycles according to preselected rules. - Evidence: parallelhelpers.py, campaignloop.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complement routine scientific manuscript reporting with a sufficiently independent inspection of the supporting research evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A manuscript may contain unsupported claims or misrepresent data even if routine paper generation and output validation pass. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A distinct evidence-audit agent judges research claims against assembled evidence, issuing pass/fail findings and requested corrections. - Decision owner: Independent audit-stage agent; parsing, hash checking and loop gates are runtime enforcement rather than the owner of the substantive audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only audit workspace, claim/evidence packet, input SHA-256 verification, validation JSON, failure-code handling and bounded repair rounds. - Closure path: Audit findings are merged into validation feedback; the paper runner revises the manuscript and reruns the audit or records incomplete status after exhaustion. - Boundary reachability: Standard shipped paper.sh calls runagenticevidenceauditgate, builds a read-only workspace and runs a separate auditor stage before accepting manuscript completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the independent audit agent leaves ordinary paper checks but removes the substantive alternative judgment over primary research evidence. - Claim being audited: Manuscript claims accurately and sufficiently reflect the experimental and source evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: Researcher&#39;s/author agent&#39;s manuscript, metrics, validation output and final evaluation. - Complementary access path: Separate read-only workspace and evidence-audit packet assembled from paper inputs and source evidence. - Independence boundary: Fresh audit stage cannot simply edit manuscript files, and hashes detect changed inputs after audit preparation; it is not only the manuscript author&#39;s self-reported score. - Who acts on findings: Paper runner converts findings into validation feedback and triggers repair or incomplete status, followed by audit rerun where allowed. - Evidence: paper.sh#L3510-L3610, paper.sh#L4042-L4090, paperevidenceaudit.py#L1819-L1879. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Detect external/future changes relevant to the research organization and adapt the harness&#39;s future operating capability in conversation with current S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New scientific papers, research directions and method changes could motivate adaptation beyond the immediate research task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped distinct adaptation-to-harness-capability loop was established; scientific idea generation belongs to the research S1 at this chosen recursion. - Decision owner: Research agent owns task-level hypotheses; researcher/developers outside this runtime choose changes to its organizational mission/capability. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Literature retrieval, idea history, agent reflection and experiment results. - Closure path: Research feedback changes individual experiment choices but no separately established external-and-prospective option is returned to whole-system present capability/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Novel idea search is the declared S1 research service, not automatically S4 adaptation of the service provider&#39;s organization. - External distinction: Available scientific literature and model/tool opportunities are task inputs, but no separate meta-organizational environmental sensing right was confirmed. - Future / prospective distinction: Future scientific opportunities could matter, but the inspected loop optimizes a bounded research campaign. - Adaptation option generated: None shown to update the harness&#39;s organizational capability at the declared recursion. - Path back into current capability / S3: None established beyond task-level experiment iteration. - Evidence: ideageneration.py, idearetryfeedback.py, campaignloop.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Establish and revise ultimate identity-level scientific policy and resolve unresolved S3/S4 tension at the research-system recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A disputed research aim, forbidden methodology or changed institution-level mandate could require ultimate policy judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party organizational constitutional/identity revision path was identified; researcher task/rubric selection is input configuration. - Decision owner: Researcher/provider policy outside this assessed harness; no qualified internal S5 or closed first-party parent authority path established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task descriptions, paper-writing instructions, configuration, validation gates and externally set parameters. - Closure path: Agent obeys existing parameters, but an identity-level dispute is not surfaced and closed through legitimate authority to govern later operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Merely changing task instructions or criteria is not legitimate ultimate-policy closure under the Profile. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Mission-level dispute, changes to what science the organization is permitted to conduct or the constitutive rules of the research harness. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: None demonstrated as an in-boundary S5 mode; external researcher/developer governance is not borrowed. - Return-to-operation path: No explicit first-party identity-level escalation and authoritative policy return was established. - Evidence: mainentry.py, paper.sh, paperfinalize.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a4bec0766c50f05633af8b583db9fdd190a70fa2/assessments/ai/ai-scientist-v3.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a4bec0766c50f05633af8b583db9fdd190a70fa2\">a4bec07 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T18:29:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openbin-ai/binny\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BINNY</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openbin-ai/binny/tree/d0c2ae4c61d222421d1505986ac6e63b7ed799fa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d0c2ae4c61d222421d1505986ac6e63b7ed799fa\">d0c2ae4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous reverse-engineering investigation and evidence-backed draft construction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown binary behaviour, contradictory strings/function evidence, tool failure, and incomplete classification or report sections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model chooses evidence queries and investigation tool calls, revises hypotheses from results, and authors findings/report sections. - Decision owner: The model actor inside the shipped Engine.Step loop, not deterministic phase transitions or the remote provider&#39;s service operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry, static sandbox, STIX/playbook prompts, objective tracking, token caps, context compaction and optional human checkpoints. - Closure path: Model call→first-party tool execution→result in e.messages→subsequent model choice; stages culminate in reviewable draft sections and findings. - Boundary reachability: The TUI&#39;s ordinary investigation command constructs the first-party engine with provider/tool registry and runs Step; no developer-authored coordinator is necessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Runtime selects budgets and legal tool boundaries, while only model reasoning selects semantically relevant operations against the sample. - Evidence: Engine Step; stage transitions; tool dispatch. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for shipped loop architecture. - Caveats: No live third-party model calls or analyst-domain correctness tests were conducted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct inter-S1 coordination function is shipped at this one-investigation recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool sequence and triage/investigate phase changes belong to one S1, not conflict between independent operational cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party ownership/adjustment of competing S1 work is exposed. - Decision owner: None for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Stage machine, token caps, project state and report objectives. - Closure path: Tool and phase feedback returns to the same model actor; no inter-unit attenuation return exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Distinct sequential phases or API tools are not distinct S1 units. - Evidence: engine, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for the reviewed distribution. - Caveats: Separate analysts can run independent processes, without a shipped cross-run coordination system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Whole-current management of distinct operational commitments/resources is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Per-task token limits, phase checkpoints and model refusals are local investigation constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No whole-current bargaining/reallocation or cross-S1 intervention right is present. - Decision owner: Analyst selects autonomy/budgets and the engine enforces caps; model selects local evidence tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxRounds, maxTokens, phase-gates, /autonomy and ContinuePastCap. - Closure path: Cap reached→local pause/analyst extension or stop; no S3 return into an operational whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Budget enforcement and human override of one analysis are not a distinct S3 function or parent-governed S3 control. - Evidence: engine caps, TUI controls. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high at the selected recursion. - Caveats: Do not infer OpenBin enterprise project management from this CLI&#39;s project selector.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: Assertions in a previously produced sample report about functions, addresses, reachability and malware/vulnerability findings. - Ordinary reporting path: The investigation model records findings and report sections during triage/investigate/synthesize, with ordinary tool results in its own context. - Complementary access path: StartQA(report) creates an on-demand skeptical review context, loads report content as claims to check rather than facts and directs fresh raw function/string/xref/reachability reads against source evidence. - Independence boundary: The Q&amp;A mode does not inherit the original evidence as trusted prior work; the model must inspect independently retrieved sample evidence. It is procedural/mode separation, not a separate institution or mandatory different provider. - Who acts on findings: Review-model feedback returns to the analyst; analyst can request recorded correction and /save a revised draft, or use the returned negative evidence for later investigation. - Function: Complementary source-based challenge of ordinary report claims beyond trusting their original narrative. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plausible but false analysis claims, unsupported reachability or section assertions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In explicitly initiated verification, the model judges the actual sampled evidence as supporting/weakening/refuting a report claim. - Decision owner: Q&amp;A model actor for evidence judgment; analyst owns case selection and final report publication, engine just transports tool results and report edits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: qaKickoff skeptical prompt, source read tools, report snapshot and draft editing/saving. - Closure path: Analyst asks to verify→separately started Q&amp;A model reads source→returns a specific finding→analyst may direct corrections/write findings→draft /save returns corrected evidence into the project&#39;s output. - Boundary reachability: /ask and StartQA are shipped user-facing operational modes, not a test fixture or maintainer-only check. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model determines whether primary evidence falsifies the report; deterministic tools merely expose bytes/functions. This credits optional agent-owned audit judgment, not autonomous selection of audit targets or remediation. - Evidence: StartQA; skeptical Q&amp;A instruction; README /ask. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium for optional complementary review topology. - Caveats: No automatic audit after every draft, provider isolation attestation, or validated remediation was observed; audit depends on an analyst asking concrete questions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No future-oriented external environmental sensing→adaptation options→present capability return is shipped. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New malware class or vulnerability questions are investigated as the current deliverable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No standing prospective redesign of BINNY&#39;s own repertoire from environmental trends is exposed. - Decision owner: Developers select playbooks/releases; model picks current-case observations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: STIX classification, playbook selection, provider switching and state persistence. - Closure path: Classifier selects an existing playbook for the current sample; this is S1 task steering, not a forward capability-adaptation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Planning and classifying the current case do not constitute externally informed S4 intelligence. - Evidence: playbooks, engine stages. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: An analyst may use results to change future practices externally; that arrangement is outside the assessed runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity/ultimate-policy closure for the agent harness is not supplied as a distinct organizational process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool risk, sample access, report publication and model-provider configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No legitimate ultimate mission/policy dispute and returned binding decision path is present. - Decision owner: Analyst/developer selects mode and tool permissions; ordinary report approval is not ultimate authority over identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sandbox restrictions, API auth, prompts, report draft gate and runtime settings. - Closure path: Permission or save choices affect current case execution/publication; no identity-level governance return is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static security/configuration and one draft approval do not establish S5, including the parent mode. - Evidence: README, sandbox, TUI. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high. - Caveats: This is not an assessment of the upstream organization or platform policies.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f849711e2035564b9de8aafcd766be6a15588b15/assessments/bi/binny.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f849711e2035564b9de8aafcd766be6a15588b15\">f849711 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T10:48:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/josh-j/pwsh-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PwshHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/josh-j/pwsh-harness/tree/e56e989bbd68d1e6a1138c3755d6c76266700a78\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e56e989bbd68d1e6a1138c3755d6c76266700a78\">e56e989 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-owned workspace operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing task requirements, file content, tool failures and shell feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select tool/arguments and revise subsequent actions or final answer. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed parent agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider adapter, tool registry, session lease, exact-action Approve or activated Auto, budget and logging enforcement. - Closure path: Tool result enters the model exchange; the next model request decides continuation; final answer and session records retain outcome. - Boundary reachability: Start-PwshHarness enables shipped agent mode; native tools and continuation are installed by the bundled agent extension. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model the dispatcher and policies retain limits but do not choose task-specific edits and repairs. - Evidence: Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentLoop.ps1; Extensions/agent/AgentTools.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentAuthority.ps1 - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for code wiring - Caveats: PowerShell 7.6/provider execution was not tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Inter-S1 interference regulation is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Read-only subagents run one at a time; shell background tasks are subprocess tools. Plans, ordered calls and session exclusivity do not demonstrate distinct operational work cells mutually adjusting a concrete conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decision/return path identified. - Decision owner: No credited metasystem owner; developer/operator configuration and agent task decisions remain separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session leases, run policies, budget enforcement, logs and ordinary approvals. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Read-only subagents run one at a time; shell background tasks are subprocess tools. Plans, ordered calls and session exclusivity do not demonstrate distinct operational work cells mutually adjusting a concrete conflict. - Evidence: Extensions/agent/AgentWork.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentLoop.ps1; Extensions/agent/AgentTasks.ps1 - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Whole-harness current control is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Token/cost/time reservations, retry fallback and user steering regulate one task under supplied limits; a model does not acquire whole-system resource/commitment authority from those gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decision/return path identified. - Decision owner: No credited metasystem owner; developer/operator configuration and agent task decisions remain separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session leases, run policies, budget enforcement, logs and ordinary approvals. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Token/cost/time reservations, retry fallback and user steering regulate one task under supplied limits; a model does not acquire whole-system resource/commitment authority from those gates. - Evidence: Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentAuthority.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentLoop.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/Budget.ps1 - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complementary challenge of a reported source defect before publication. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A parent model can mistake a guard/caller/library premise and publish a false or unsupported finding. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge whether the concrete claimed defect is supported, refuted, reproduced or unresolved after direct source/counterevidence inspection. - Decision owner: Fresh read-only checker model invoked by reportfinding; runtime parses/records the judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded readonly schemas, fresh provider exchange, exact file/line/trigger/consequence, observed-command provenance and completion policy. - Closure path: Invoke-AgentFindingCheck returns verdict/premise/counterevidence to the parent; Get-AgentFindingReview rejects citing refuted findings and requests checked/qualified reporting in a further step. - Boundary reachability: Bundled AgentTools installs work schemas and completion-review; AgentPresentation dispatches reportfinding to Invoke-AgentFindingCheck; Engine Delegate creates the separate read-only exchange. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model tests the disputed premise using callers/tests/guards, while deterministic parsing and answer reminders only enforce its evidence labels. Generic logs and routine parser/Pester checks are not credited. - Evidence: Extensions/agent/AgentWork.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentLoop.ps1; Extensions/agent/AgentTools.ps1; Extensions/agent/AgentPresentation.ps1 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for this bounded audit mode - Caveats: Parent selects the claim and both actors may share provider/model. Independence is fresh reasoning with separate direct source access, not institutional independence; no live witness or universal claim coverage. - Claim being audited: Parent&#39;s reported defect at File/Line with Trigger/Consequence and optional executed reproduction. - Ordinary reporting path: Parent&#39;s own source interpretation and draft final finding. - Complementary access path: Fresh checker reads cited code, callers, guards and tests through independently selected readonly tools and searches for refutation. - Independence boundary: New base messages and separate provider cache/replay, readonly actor, no nested delegates; it receives the claim rather than the parent&#39;s full reasoning transcript. - Who acts on findings: Parent model revises/qualifies its finding; completion-review returns contradictions into subsequent operation when a step/time budget remains.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: External/prospective capability adaptation is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Lessons summarize past workspace command success; /extend implements a directly requested extension. Neither path supplies environmental/future modelling that develops and selects an adaptation option. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decision/return path identified. - Decision owner: No credited metasystem owner; developer/operator configuration and agent task decisions remain separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session leases, run policies, budget enforcement, logs and ordinary approvals. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Lessons summarize past workspace command success; /extend implements a directly requested extension. Neither path supplies environmental/future modelling that develops and selects an adaptation option. - Evidence: Extensions/agent/AgentLessons.ps1; Extensions/self-extend/Commands.ps1; Extensions/self-extend/Authoring.ps1 - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity/ultimate-policy closure is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Live Auto activation, source-bound extension approval and risk capability labels constrain ordinary task/extension execution without addressing system identity or balancing S3/S4. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decision/return path identified. - Decision owner: No credited metasystem owner; developer/operator configuration and agent task decisions remain separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session leases, run policies, budget enforcement, logs and ordinary approvals. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Live Auto activation, source-bound extension approval and risk capability labels constrain ordinary task/extension execution without addressing system identity or balancing S3/S4. - Evidence: Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentAuthority.ps1; Extensions/self-extend/Approval.ps1; Extensions/self-extend/Commands.ps1 - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/17bf101ef55129f7f992cd21e1a1cf5b49b9b154/assessments/pw/pwshharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 17bf101ef55129f7f992cd21e1a1cf5b49b9b154\">17bf101 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T19:23:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Oswald-Agent/Oswald\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OSWALD</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Oswald-Agent/Oswald/tree/5a2d6103c4c48735a909fd5ee78b0362fcbee993\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5a2d6103c4c48735a909fd5ee78b0362fcbee993\">5a2d610 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute software-change work by interpreting a task, inspecting context, modifying code, generating/testing acceptance evidence and deciding whether the resulting patch is adequate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable user requests and codebases, code/tool/test failures, ambiguous specifications and review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Planner&#39;s task plan, Coder&#39;s source edits and next tool calls, Tester&#39;s test construction/execution, and Reviewer&#39;s acceptance or blocking critique within the task. - Decision owner: distinct provider-backed role agents invoked in the first-party shipped CLI; model responses choose content and tool actions. The deterministic orchestrator bounds the lifecycle; the human still owns final external publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: immutable worktree base, role-scoped tools, sandbox, artifact schema/persistence, stage timeout and two-attempt cap. - Closure path: Planner spec → Coder model/tool edits → Tester authored/executed tests → Reviewer model verdict → success or findings returned to Coder for a second attempt, or reported stop with saved patch. - Boundary reachability: the default local CLI explicitly constructs all four agent modules and runs them through Orchestrator; it is not an example-only or future-service path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: plans, edits, tests and review judgments are model-authored; deterministic stage gates implement those decisions rather than originate task-specific discretion. - Evidence: CLI roles, Planner model/tools, Coder tool loop, Tester commands, orchestrator corrective feedback. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for shipped source and decision path; no live LLM/provider run performed. - Caveats: fake test adapters and architecture diagrams do not attest to model quality or autonomous PR publication; operator must separately inspect/apply the saved patch.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct inter-S1 interference-attenuation loop established at the declared one-task boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: compatibility between implementation, tests and review comments, rather than independently competing S1 units with an explicit oscillation/conflict regulation relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: role outputs and correction findings are routed in a fixed sequence; no separately established S2 right mediates a specific inter-unit disturbance. - Decision owner: role agents own stage-level S1 choices and the deterministic pipeline sequences handoffs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed artifact handoffs, stage ordering, test/review gates and bounded retry. - Closure path: a failed test or blocked review returns to the Coder inside the same task loop, not to two or more independently active S1 units through S2 coordination. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: four named agents and a correction edge are not evidence of a separate interference-attenuation function; no first-party inter-S1 mutual adjustment witness is supplied. - Evidence: fixed pipeline and retry, role binding. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported local single-task CLI. - Caveats: separately operated Oswald processes or future web workers are outside the reviewed first-party S2 boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no first-party whole-system discretionary current-control loop over a portfolio of operational commitments/resources is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stage failure, timeout, unapproved code, worktree and sandbox cleanup, not dynamic whole-system resource allocation or accountable priority conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: stage progression, retry and stop conditions are fixed in code; model actors can only make task-stage decisions; the operator controls the invocation and external publication. - Decision owner: developer predefines lifecycle/limits; orchestrator enforces them; human retains final integration decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run status/metrics, stagetimeout, two-attempt correction, sandbox isolation, report persistence, cancellation and cleanup. - Closure path: deterministic gate/stop and report/patch return, not a discretionary whole-system current-control decision returning to alter a multi-S1 operational portfolio. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the central Orchestrator is named like a manager but its current control is a fixed single-task state machine; progress telemetry does not transfer whole-system regulatory authority to an agent. - Evidence: lifecycle/limits, stop, retry and status, recorded run/metrics. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for current CLI boundary. - Caveats: human patch review and planned fleet services are not S3 parent modes in the shipped pipeline.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently examine the Coder&#39;s proposed source changes and Tester evidence for acceptance against a specification, with a blocking corrective verdict. - Disturbance / variety regulated: silent implementation defects, self-reported completion or tests that fail to support claimed acceptance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the separate Reviewer model chooses APPROVED, CHANGESREQUESTED or rejection with blocking findings against the cumulative source diff, plan and tests. - Decision owner: ReviewerAgent model in the shipped role wiring; the orchestrator validates the verdict and deterministically applies the gate, rather than authoring review judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh manager.getdiff() captured after Tester edits, immutable reviewchanges artifact, read-only Reviewer tool boundary, typed verdict consistency checks and correction cap. - Closure path: cumulative worktree diff plus test evidence → distinct read-only Reviewer model judgment → orchestrator veto or returned findings → Coder correction attempt/terminal fail, with review and patch saved for human inspection. - Boundary reachability: the default CLI always assembles and invokes ReviewerAgent after actual test execution; the diff is freshly read from the worktree, not merely accepted from the Coder&#39;s own completion narrative. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Reviewer produces substantive model-authored blocking issues, while deterministic checks reject internally inconsistent approvals; authoring and audit roles have different inputs/permissions. - Claim being audited: Coder&#39;s source change satisfies the Planner&#39;s specified work and is supported by tests. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder-produced CodeChanges and Tester-produced TestResults passed into the main pipeline. - Complementary access path: direct cumulative worktree diff via WorktreeManager.getdiff() and separate read-only model inspection of spec, changes and testresults. - Independence boundary: Reviewer is a distinct model invocation/role with no write or tool permissions, evaluates actual patch and test artifacts rather than relying solely on Coder status; not an external independent reviewer, separate provider, or independently reexecuted tests. - Who acts on findings: the orchestrator returns review blocks to Coder for at most one correction, or refuses success and preserves the terminal report. - Evidence: fresh diff and Reviewer gate, read-only model-authored verdict, reviewer composition. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium for installed complementary review/feedback topology; no real provider run or measured audit efficacy. - Caveats: audit sees produced test results and patch rather than obtaining separate runtime probes; independence is role/access separation at source level, not an organizationally independent third-party semantic review. No automatic merge authority follows.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external/prospective sensing → adaptation choice → durable current-capability change loop demonstrated in the supported CLI. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unpredictable current software task and its failing tests are handled in S1, not future technology/environment adaptation of Oswald itself. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Planner chooses task implementation approach, not a strategic future capability portfolio; no S4 adaptation owner or returned decision is wired. - Decision owner: user selects tasks and maintainers evolve Oswald outside assessed runtime; the agent pipeline cannot promote its patch into Oswald capability autonomously. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task plan, correction findings, saved reports/patch, model-provider configuration and developer roadmap. - Closure path: within-task corrections may improve a candidate patch but do not produce an external-and-future adaptation judgment applied to the harness&#39;s current operational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: code planning on a supplied request and a human-applied patch are local S1 work, not S4; planned architecture is adjacent. - Evidence: Planner request boundary, no-publish pipeline, future architecture disclaimer. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for supported local mode. - Caveats: third-party users can ask Oswald to implement strategic tooling, but this alone does not give the harness a self-directed S4 decision loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no supported runtime loop for ultimate purpose/identity-policy conflict and authoritative closure at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary task request constraints, sandbox restrictions and code publication risk, rather than identity/charter tensions between inside-and-now and outside-and-then. - Decisive decision or feedback right: task constraints are supplied by the human and enforced by runtime; no identity/ultimate-policy matter is routed to a legitimate parent decision and returned as a governing choice. - Decision owner: user/maintainers choose task, models, sandbox and external patch disposition, but their role is not a first-party closed S5 mode of the task runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configuration, role prompts, task schema, tool access, test/review gates and human post-run patch review. - Closure path: approval/denial and failure return to the local task, not a policy-identity decision governing the future purpose of Oswald. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed prompts, sandbox/tool restrictions and a human hold before Git mutation are ordinary constraints; neither agent nor parent returns a runtime identity decision. - Evidence: CLI config and role wiring, README human VCS boundary, no commit/merge orchestration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported CLI. - Caveats: upstream OSS maintainer governance is adjacent, and the target end-state human approval API is not credited as an installed S5 parent mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/03af5b02eb68d24c6ad4fbda9aa842fcbd4c309d/assessments/os/oswald.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 03af5b02eb68d24c6ad4fbda9aa842fcbd4c309d\">03af5b0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T03:22:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/google-research/harness2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness²</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/google-research/harness2/tree/1cfe254e8d25b15f37048c3730134686abe60335\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1cfe254e8d25b15f37048c3730134686abe60335\">1cfe254 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out the present task&#39;s harness-editing and probing work through autonomous model-chosen candidate changes, actual executor rollouts and evidence-informed candidate selection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unseen tasks, insufficient baseline harness behavior, different candidate tool/prompt/skill modifications, executable errors and heterogeneous task deliverables. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured proposer model chooses what edits to write inside the allowed harness tree; selector model chooses among concrete completed candidate outputs using execution evidence. Those chosen changes are validated and run by the shipped orchestration, with later candidate/edit choices exposed to resulting observations. - Decision owner: Model-backed Proposer/selector operating through first-party runstage, runmode and runselection; external model/runtime implementations supply inference and task execution but do not replace first-party decision/feedback routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Python stage scheduler, editable tree validation, candidate/run budget ledger, real adapter execution, retry/fallback, evidence-safe render, filesystem result records and selection persistence. - Closure path: Task/base rollout → proposal-agent harness edit → first-party tree accept/reject → substrate probe with new tree → observed deliverable/trajectory → independent composition/selection decision → chosen current-task output and selection record. - Boundary reachability: Public CLI runsweep → rundomain → runtask → runmode → runstage/real candidate adapter execution → runselection is shipped source. Proposer and selector may be model-backed via released Proposer.call implementations rather than sample-only DemoProposer. No external task executor cognitive function is claimed as a Harness² internal actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: First-party code gives model-backed editor/selector discretion that changes candidate harnesses and selects from observed task outcomes; deterministic scheduler, validator and external benchmark judge are only enforcement/evidence mechanisms. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/proposer.py. - Basis: Reachable configured proposal/edit/probe/compare/select source loop for current task. - Confidence: High for structural mode reachability; no model-backed external benchmark execution attested. - Caveats: Model-less deterministic demo is not itself autonomous; the positive applies only to the supported configured model editor/selector with functional substrate. Absolute benchmark gains are not used as VSM classification evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No independent S2 feedback mechanism stabilizes specific interference between separately viable S1 operating units at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel task candidates and concurrent domains can compete for execution resources or diverge, but running k proposals/rollouts is controlled experimental scheduling rather than detected inter-S1 organizational conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ThreadPoolExecutor widths, domain ordering, mode-separated namespaces and shared candidate-pool aggregation control concurrency/experiment identity. No agent makes inter-operating-division stabilization decisions that feed back to competing S1 units. - Decision owner: Deterministic task/domain scheduler and user-specified budgets/workers, not a model-owned S2 coordination actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Candidate labels, per-mode Namespace, thread pools, deterministic domain order, cost ledger and output directory partitioning. - Closure path: Proposals/rollouts execute and join → composition/selection. Missing a sensed cross-S1 interference → specific adaptive damping judgment → returned adjustments to each affected autonomous S1 operating division. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task stages and domain jobs are parts of one harness-optimization experiment; concurrent task execution and result aggregation do not automatically establish S2 among independent viable operating organizations. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/ledger.py. - Basis: Bounded source negative after identifying all shipped within-task and across-domain concurrency mechanisms. - Confidence: High for reviewed default modes. - Caveats: An external experiment orchestrator or multi-tenant platform could add S2, but neither is part of the assessed frozen first-party distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct whole-current organization control loop assigns and revises commitments/resources/priorities over multiple independent S1 operating domains. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task failures, budgets and experimental queue pressure are managed at run scope; no whole-system operational obligations spanning independent current S1 divisions are established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Scheduler selects the prespecified task order/mode/budget and handles per-task failures; model selector chooses a task artifact; neither makes a global current-control intervention among distinct operating units. - Decision owner: CLI operator for configuration, deterministic Python scheduler for execution and model editor/selector for task results. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Namespace-separated runs, BudgetLedger, result statuses, resume, checked candidate trees and domain task streaming. - Closure path: Configured tasks → bounded execution/selection/summary. Missing whole-organization status → legitimate binding current priority/resource/commitment decision → intervention returned into separate S1 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Select-best-harness for one task is S1; allocating worker count by human CLI is configuration. Neither is an autonomous or first-party S3 decision over a viable multi-unit current organization. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/ledger.py; harness2/core/selection.py. - Basis: Boundary-relative bounded negative separating experiment management from organization current control. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Research lab/program management may be external; it is not an owned Harness² control function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit obtains separate operational reality evidence, challenges S3 assertions and returns authorized corrective S3 action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Candidate harnesses can fail or produce inferior outcomes, and benchmark grader information could leak. First-party tree validation, contrastive probing and independent post-run judge help task-level evidence integrity but do not audit organizational S3 performance claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Code validates a tree and denies grader-verdict leakage; a selector judges delivered candidate outputs while an external rubric judge scores outputs in a separate stage. None reports independently sourced whole-current operating contradictions to a legitimate S3 manager with binding correction. - Decision owner: First-party task/evidence validator and choice-model for the current improvement task; external benchmark judge for scoring, not a separate audited management officer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Editable-tree validator, forbidden-grader evidence checks, candidate pool anonymization, external judgeone, cost ledger and summary artifacts. - Closure path: Candidate output/trajectory → validation and selection/benchmark score → task/result record; missing S3 current report → independent operating observation → complementary challenge → authorized corrective management return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Contrastive behavioral evidence and result-score separation are real, but output selection for one task is neither S3 evidence independence nor an audit owner with a corrective cross-system path. - Evidence: harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/judge.py; harness2/core/context.py; harness2/core/ledger.py. - Basis: Bounded negative after explicitly considering the grader/selector/evidence-control positive candidate. - Confidence: Medium-high within the frozen app. - Caveats: A dedicated independently observable production system auditor could be built externally; no such shipped owner and returned S3 correction were shown.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Learn from current encountered environments and adopted task-level harness modifications to autonomously maintain a reusable future-facing improvement library that influences subsequent harness-design decisions across new tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-world tasks arrive that were not known at initial harness construction; improvements for one problem may not transfer directly. Cross-task domain patterns, changing successful component choices and observed errors must inform future harness proposals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: After actual current-task rollouts, the model-backed selector picks an adopted harness from candidate evidence and model-backed reflectadoption distills transferable experience. First-party Library.add accepts that model-selected result/lesson into durable per-domain library, and subsequent proposer/editor sessions read it for future harness reconfiguration. - Decision owner: Configured autonomous selector + experience-reflecting model agent inside the shipped runtask/rundomain loop. Deterministic file/library code persists and presents the agent&#39;s chosen strategic material, not an external human deciding each adopted lesson. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Domain task stream, mode-separated Library, selected harness tree and adoption metadata, contrastive trajectory record, EXPERIENCE.md and INDEX.md, context injection, safe tree validation, leak prevention and retaining only earlier tasks. - Closure path: Environment/task rollouts → model selection of accepted harness → model distills evidence-supported transferable future lesson → library persists full adopted harness + rationale → next task imports library into new model proposer context → its operational harness edits can change under accumulated past experience. - Boundary reachability: A first-party public runsweep invokes rundomain, creates Library objects per mode/domain and executes tasks sequentially. runonemode calls reflectadoption and, with evidence-rich experience, modelib.add; runstage passes librarydir to context.build which actually copies old library context into the next proposer session. No bespoke adopter glue is needed for this supported released mode. - External distinction: Novel tasks in the same domain arrive after prior harnesses have been accepted; actual task-execution contrasts, error patterns and domain-specific cues are fed back through the prior-task experience library rather than assumed from static program configuration. - Future / prospective distinction: The reflector distils a transferable cue/lesson intended for later unknown tasks, and Library stores adopted trees and why they were chosen; this is not the immediate selected deliverable alone. - Adaptation option generated: An autonomous proposer/editor generates new harness-component edits based on stored prior domain experience, and an autonomous selector/reflection loop chooses, explains and retains the successful candidate tree and lesson to inform later alternative edits. - Path back into current capability / S3: Library.add persists the chosen tree and lesson, context.build copies the resulting library into the next proposer work directory, and recursion.runstage hands it to the model edit session whose validated candidate modifies the next task&#39;s executable harness. There is no separate whole-current S3 manager, so the concrete return is direct into next S1 harness-improvement capability at this recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent makes substantive adoption and transferable-experience decisions from observed external task outcomes; first-party memory storage/return closes them into future edit decisions. This is a prospective reusable improvement-harness capability, distinct from current task&#39;s best-output selection alone. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/library.py; harness2/core/library.py; harness2/core/context.py; harness2/core/recursion.py. - Basis: Installed agent-owned adoption/reflection and persistent cross-task future-harness change feedback, independent of reported benchmark gains. - Confidence: Medium-high for the supported configured cross-task learning mode; effectiveness/generalization not independently tested. - Caveats: This is S4 of the declared harness-improvement organization, not S4 of a separately deployed fixed target coding assistant. Individual future tasks begin from a base harness but the stored adopted earlier trees and model lessons are available to the editing proposer; this is guidance-mediated capability adaptation, not unconditional promotion of a new globally deployed runtime. A failed or empty reflection prevents library admission, so not every task advances S4.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate legitimate mission/identity-policy arbitration for the harness-improvement organization is shipped as a first-party closed decision path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User-selected benchmark, base model, allowed editable components, executor, task scope and no-leak policies bound optimization but do not entail an identity/purpose governance dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: CLI/research operator fixes the task stream, model/substrate budget and source boundaries; validators enforce prechosen component constraints. No first-party actor chooses legitimate ultimate aims or changes governing ethical purpose and returns those decisions. - Decision owner: External operator/research institution for mission and boundary setup; first-party deterministic config/validation has no identity-level authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Benchmarks, CLI config, read-only rubric protection, editable-component allowlist, ledger and model/executor selection. - Closure path: Configured objective/limits → task improvements in those limits; missing legitimate ultimate policy/identity alternatives → authoritative S5 choice → returned system-wide changed governing purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Technical safety and user objective configuration are narrower than governing what the system is and what its ultimate purpose should be. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/spec.py; harness2/core/tree.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; docs/EXPERIMENTS.md. - Basis: Bounded no-first-party identity-purpose governance after inspecting all admissibility and external authority surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Google Research or the deployer may separately exercise organizational S5; not imported as a Harness² runtime ownership claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9a20d73e3efcee2bcc1639c7365c3b178e155f74/assessments/harness2.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9a20d73e3efcee2bcc1639c7365c3b178e155f74\">9a20d73 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-17T23:54:50+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/0xfe-project/eta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">eta</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-17\">2026-11-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/0xfe-project/eta/tree/6b62da886b225391a157c7486e93b85aef73a17c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6b62da886b225391a157c7486e93b85aef73a17c\">6b62da8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Configurable model-backed agents solve owner-assigned tasks with selected tools and feedback into subsequent model turns. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Requests and tool results vary during a durable conversation/task execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The runtime agent chooses responses and tool calls, and may submit finishtask or request clarification. - Decision owner: Model-backed task-executing agent(s), including Captain in its own operational sessions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kernel Runner, typed tool middleware, RunPump wakeups, per-task sessions, task execution journal and restart controls. - Closure path: A provider tool call is executed, its result is fed into the next model turn; the agent completes or blocks tasks and resulting card/activity updates wake consumers. - Boundary reachability: The standard host runs bundled eta-core Runner and eta-harness RunPump, and exposes task agents via shipped TaskRunner and Board tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is actual model-conditioned task execution, not only a UI dispatcher or static script. - Evidence: runner.ts, pump.ts, runner.ts - Basis: explicit first-party source, tests -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate concrete dependency and workspace-interference among distinct task-executing agent S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A dependent agent can start from an unfinished/inconsistent predecessor&#39;s worktree, or a cancelled predecessor can leave unsatisfiable dependent work; cyclic task dependencies can deadlock execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide whether a dependent run may enter the machine queue and what Git base to use; reject impossible dependency edges and propagate breakage. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic TaskGraph/TaskBoard and task-runner pump; the collaborating agents and owner supply their dependency/task graph, not this policy&#39;s runtime judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskGraph gate/deriveBase/checkEdge/derive, TaskRunner waitsForDeps/pump, Board dependency mutation and UI notifications. - Closure path: A waiting dependent agent consumes no slot; completion releases it with the derived predecessor branch/HEAD; cancellation marks it blocked or withdraws the run until user fixes the edge. - Boundary reachability: Shipped TaskRunner queries Board/TaskGraph before admitting distinct agent task runs, and first-party task-graph tests exercise dependency waiting, cancellations and derived base rather than describing hypothetical configuration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The credited operational disturbance is conflicting dependent work over shared git/workspace state and broken upstream commitments, not mere group messaging or task ordering. - Evidence: graph.ts, runner.ts, task-graph.test.ts - Basis: first-party source and native test cases - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Requires explicitly declared dependencies and standard project worktree task mode; this is not general semantic negotiation between unrelated agents. - Distinct S1 units: Two independently model-backed task-agent executors handling predecessor Task A and dependent Task B in one shared project. - Inter-S1 disturbance: B&#39;s workspace/run can refer to A&#39;s uncommitted or cancelled work; cycles can block both agents and a wrong base can erase predecessor progress. - Attenuating coordination relation: Dependency graph refuses cycles, withholds dependent first runs, resolves only eligible predecessor statuses and derives inherited worktree base; broken dependencies block user intervention. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The queue skips B until A resolves, picks a source branch/HEAD when B begins and marks B blocked if A is cancelled, changing what subsequent agent turns execute. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: There is a structurally evidenced cross-agent working-tree/dependency interference and a concrete fail-closed gate/base reconciliation path that prevents wrong concurrent action.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Captain observes active agents, task failures, blocked work and queues and can choose current-work interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The owner&#39;s active agent team can stall, fail, miss deadlines, leave unanswered Inbox questions or require priority/assignment correction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Captain decides how to instruct existing member agents, continue or stop active tasks and relay unresolved questions; the automatic supervisor only detects and presents disturbances. - Decision owner: Model-backed Captain system agent with bundled S3-facing tools; user controls activation/settings but does not make each Captain supervisory choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Supervisor tick/observeReviews, task/inbox state, Captain listagents/listtasks/assigningroup/controltask and persistent review inbox. - Closure path: Supervisor-created review is delivered to Captain as a session turn; Captain can inspect the live state and send an operational reassignment or control action, and task state/agent work changes as the result. - Boundary reachability: Captain is an actual protected system agent with first-party model sessions and action tools. First-party tests cover failure/stuck-task review delivery, agent authorization and task continuation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic failure detection, retry limits and scheduling enforce constraints but do not substitute for Captain&#39;s agent decision on what intervention to make; no autonomous managerial behavior is inferred from &#39;Captain&#39; label alone. - Evidence: service.ts, supervisor.ts, tools.ts, captain.test.ts - Basis: explicit source and first-party test scenarios - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Requires Captain model configured and supervisor enabled. Owner may intervene via UI, but distinct first-party parent S3 mode has not been reconstructed sufficiently to claim A(P). - Whole-system current view: Captain reads agent roster/status, assignments, task board and Inbox across a shared workbench; supervisor consolidates failures, stale runs and reviews. - Current-control decision scope: Model-backed Captain may assign and reassign active team commitments, control tasks and escalate a stuck Inbox item for a human answer, with resulting task-run updates.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit authority with a separate operational access path and corrective closure was found in the supported workbench. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Normal session logs and a supervisor&#39;s own status reports could hide incorrect agent or Captain claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An independent actor would need to inspect separate evidence and return a corrective challenge to S3. - Decision owner: No separate first-party complementary auditor identified; ordinary agent, Captain and unit tests are not independence witnesses. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CaptainAudit append-only operations JSONL, timeline, ordinary supervisor checks and tests. - Closure path: Logs/review inbox reflect the same active system reports; no independent evidence acquisition, challenge judgment or separate audit findings loop was located. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A trace of normal team events, self-review and developer testing are not independently controlled audit access. - Evidence: audit.ts, supervisor.ts, captain.test.ts - Basis: bounded negative assessment of available first-party surfaces - Confidence: medium - Caveats: A user could audit the SQLite store manually, but that possibility is not a shipped independent audit feedback loop. ### Absence scope - Surfaces inspected: Captain operations audit JSONL, supervisor, review observer, direct/team timelines, host session journals, task-graph tests and external/runtime architecture. - Plausible first-party paths checked: Separate auditor role or adapter, complementary probe with independent evidence, operational finding to Captain action/recovery path. - Why no material first-party path remains: Reviewed paths produce ordinary self-observation and logging, not independent complementary checking of operational truth and a returned corrective decision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Future-capability adaptation plausibly exists through Captain proposals for new agents, groups, routines and revisions, but its full external/prospective evidence chain remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Novel external work or changed model/tool conditions may require different future roles, capabilities or ongoing schedules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A prospective option would have to be chosen against future environmental needs, approved and made available to subsequent work. - Decision owner: Captain can suggest new agents/groups/routines and the owner confirms; whether Captain&#39;s shipped model path actually closes external-and-then intelligence is not independently established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Captain proposals registry, provider/models listing, user-confirmation callback, initial agent/persona construction and routine brief editing. - Closure path: The approval callback validates and applies new capacity and notifies Captain; missing is proof that a distinct future/environment analysis produced the adaptation option rather than a straightforward user command. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Do not promote generic agent spawning, routine editing or making requested work into S4 without evidence of an externally informed prospective option loop. - Evidence: proposals.ts, propose-agent.ts, runner.ts, service.ts - Basis: first-party prospective path exists but specific causality uncertain - Confidence: medium-low - Caveats: Targeted re-review should trace a production Captain decision from changed external evidence to an accepted adaptation option and then to changed capability. Abstract future extension is not the uncertainty: these concrete production proposals are.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Retain the workbench&#39;s ultimate agent identity/base instruction policy under the owner, returning approved changes to subsequent sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent-originated changes to the global behavioral base or the privileged Captain identity can redefine the workbench&#39;s operating mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Approve or reject a change to base instruction policy and Captain&#39;s protected prompt/identity; prohibit unapproved mutation. - Decision owner: Legitimate human operator via Captain&#39;s first-party pending-operation/decideOperation flow, not Captain&#39;s own judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Base prompt version history, protected setprompt operation, setPrompt guard, proposal state machine, prompt getter/host session composition and approval tests. - Closure path: A requested base/Captain prompt mutation records a pending operation; user approval revalidates and applies versioned content, which is loaded for subsequent model sessions, while rejection leaves policy unchanged. - Boundary reachability: Captain tools for privileged prompts create approval-required operations and the shipped Captain plugin exposes decideOperation; service guards privileged prompt edits, and tests assert protected prompt versions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is parent-only ultimate identity/behavioral charter custody, not S5 credit from an ordinary per-tool approval. No autonomous S5 decision mode is established. - Evidence: base.ts, service.ts, tools.ts, captain.test.ts - Basis: first-party protected authority and return path - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Policy scope is the default agent behavioral charter and Captain&#39;s privileged system instructions; ordinary user changes to task status are S3/S1, not S5. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Altering the global base system prompt that all agents inherit, or protected Captain system instructions, changes who this workbench directs agents to be and their overarching behavioral mandate. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Human workbench owner approves or rejects the first-party pending Captain operation; no independently justified agent-owned S5 mode is claimed. - Return-to-operation path: An accepted version is stored and served through basePrompt()/session composition to later agents; refusal preserves prior policy and reports the outcome to Captain.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aae4e7e575b34709555fd08cc9d00d0ba87cbb30/assessments/et/eta.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aae4e7e575b34709555fd08cc9d00d0ba87cbb30\">aae4e7e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T00:03:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/inhere/suga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Suga</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/inhere/suga/tree/88916d5d23e974f7c02c9dd37f6d02f05b681a31\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 88916d5d23e974f7c02c9dd37f6d02f05b681a31\">88916d5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce coding/investigation outcomes through the model/tool loop, optionally using read-only research/exploration actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requests, file and command results, tool refusals, provider errors and evolving task observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select next tools, delegate exploration, interpret observations and decide the answer or next work. - Decision owner: The configured model-backed principal and its operational child actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider adapters, persistence, workspace/approval guards, transcript/context projection and limits. - Closure path: Model request → chosen tool/agent action → persisted results or child report → next model request/final answer. - Boundary reachability: Current wiring instantiates the first-party kernel and tool set for CLI/ACP; enabled agent tooling builds real read-only child runners rather than borrowing an uninstantiated framework. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model actors leaves storage, permissions and transport but no task-specific discretionary choices. - Evidence: [Kernel][R1], [current wiring][R2], [child construction][R3], [agent tool][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No live inference or agent E2E execution was run. Operational quality and benchmark results are not inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: The local peer-chat protocol attenuates repeated automatic replies between independent workcells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: One session&#39;s peer reply can wake another, whose response wakes the first again without new human work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the anti-reply hop policy and when a held exchange may resume. - Decision owner: The developer/operator supplies maxhops and the continuation policy; the runtime counts/holds. An autonomous coordination-policy actor is not supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sender attribution, TurnHops + 1, configured receiving threshold, held-message queue, message IDs/status and per-turn send cap. - Closure path: Peer reply advances hop depth → receiver reaches maxhops → no new turn is started → held state/status is observable and the message enters only a later human-started turn. - Boundary reachability: The shipped sessionmessage tool is available by default outside disabled/Gofer-job modes, and the real local chat socket delivery path applies the hold rule. This is a first-party anti-oscillation path, not a hypothetical custom mailbox. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model may choose a message, but the decisive coordination threshold/hold rule remains configured; C records the S2-specific construction/control surface rather than autonomous ownership. - Evidence: [Native peer tool][R9], [host message construction][R10], [hop context][R11], [receiver hold][R12], [configuration][R13]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Older peer versions may not support hop gating; the tool reports that limitation. This path does not provide shared-file reservations or coordination for arbitrary external runtimes.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the principal workgroup&#39;s current background operational commitments, including independent subagent tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Subagents, long commands or watches may remain running, fail, return unhelpful work or consume the current workgroup&#39;s time/resources after priorities change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review the current owned-task set, read progress/output, choose which work continues or is killed, and revise subsequent operational action. - Decision owner: Base mode: the principal model-backed agent. Parent mode: the local user supervising that same session/task organization through chat. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session-scoped task manager, task snapshots/output/termination, agent run metadata, task notices, cancellation/process-tree handling and configured budget/time ceilings. - Closure path: Task list/output/notice → principal or operator selects current intervention → task kill/cancellation and resulting snapshot/notice → subsequent model work proceeds with revised commitments. - Boundary reachability: CLI/run/chat/ACP wiring installs native task controls when the first-party task manager is present; agent background runs use that manager. Chat&#39;s :tasks/-k commands directly expose the same session task state and termination path to the user. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses whether to retain or terminate current owned work; the manager only executes the choice. Configured hard limits do not own those discretionary decisions. Human supervision is separately evidenced, rather than inferred from write approval. - Evidence: [Tool current-view/intervention][R5], [manager List/Kill][R6], [model feedback][R7], [chat view/cancellation][R8], [task wiring][R23], [actor construction][R3]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Scope is one principal session&#39;s owned background workgroup. This does not establish a regulator over all independent chats or external Gofer jobs; a lone foreground run need not exercise this mode.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No first-party audit-specific independent judgment with corrective current-control return is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect completion claims, missed tests, malformed output or defects in produced artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No installed independent audit owner adjudicates an operational claim through a complementary channel. - Decision owner: The producer and configured delivery checks handle ordinary validation; explore/research children are general operational helpers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Caller-selected --verify command, fixed nudge rules, JSON-schema checks, traces, raw transcript access and read-only child tools. - Closure path: Failed delivery checks re-enter the same producing session. There is no supplied independent audit judgment/authority loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic schema/nudge/verification and generic child exploration are not complementary S3. The config/agents reviewer sketch is not one of the current tool&#39;s instantiated actor types. - Evidence: [Delivery verification][R14], [nudge][R15], [output contract][R16], [actual child types][R3], [agent interface][R4], [current/archive boundary][R21]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A caller can compose an independent reviewer around direct evidence, but that application is not supplied by the reviewed current product.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: External research results, user feedback and long-session context pressure are operational inputs rather than a supplied future-capability model. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No intelligence actor develops adaptation alternatives and closes their return into present capability/current control. - Decision owner: The principal performs requested research and writes permitted memory; user/maintainer selects configuration and releases. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Web research child, feedback/reference memory kinds, summaries, handoff/context segments, idle consolidation and provider settings. - Closure path: Stored memory and compressed context enter later requests; no prospective adaptation-option-to-capability decision path is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remembering corrections, summarizing history or doing a current research task does not by itself establish S4. No function-specific parent adaptation loop is evidenced. - Evidence: [Research construction][R3], [memory controls][R19], [idle consolidation][R17], [segment break][R18], [product scope][R20]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Developer optimization/evaluation and archived learning/self-loop documents are not runtime product owners.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication-and-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Write permissions, external-origin taint, protected paths, agent instructions and memory confirmation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level matter is specifically routed to legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a governing policy decision. - Decision owner: User/developer supplies prompt, approval and tool/memory rules; agents own bounded local action choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only registration, strongest approval rules, dangerous-action confirmation, taint guards, rules loading and session state. - Closure path: Allow/deny/confirm outcomes govern actions or memory writes, not ultimate identity or S3–S4 tension resolution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static rules, user approval and a memory kind called user/feedback are not S5. The narrower user current-task supervision supports S3 only. - Evidence: [Wiring and approval assembly][R2], [child policy snapshot][R3], [memory confirmation][R19], [documented permissions][R20]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The public project/development organization and external Gofer governance are outside this product-level assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8068db51edb8053bfef30d194229b694a2d36b55/assessments/su/suga.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8068db51edb8053bfef30d194229b694a2d36b55\">8068db5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T00:22:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/djtoon/pi-forge\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pi-Forge</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/djtoon/pi-forge/tree/3af0d73a0f45701a4b1324d3b3bb272bc312ef2e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3af0d73a0f45701a4b1324d3b3bb272bc312ef2e\">3af0d73 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-backed Forge and generated pi agents perform their user-delegated building or domain work by selecting tools and adapting responses to results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User demands, current workspace state, unavailable credentials, files, tool failures and environment/domain data. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide what to ask, read, write, run, validate or explain on subsequent model turns within available tools and policy. - Decision owner: The active pi model session in the supported real-provider harness run; user defines goals and guardrails. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runHarness, pi session/tool APIs, forge-tools, custom/skills, model settings, guard hooks, CLI/web/RPC transport and plan UI. - Closure path: User/domain request → model chooses tool and arguments → first-party tool executes or denies → result/observation returns to model for next step. - Boundary reachability: forge CLI launches a generated first-party harness in web/TUI and documented generated harness commands expose the same model-tool loop in headless/RPC modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without model choice, the tools and generator can be called directly but do not decide how to fulfill an open-ended user request. - Evidence: launcher, harness core, Forge tools, interview skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Requires configured model provider; static source analysis does not certify model results, user-approval behavior or build success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party S2-specific anti-interference loop across distinct concurrently operating agent units was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Main and builder sessions might contend for files or user interaction; multiple generated harnesses might share host resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No independent actor identifies and resolves particular cross-S1 interference through an adjustment returned to both units. - Decision owner: The server routes channels and reload events; users may direct sessions, but there is no explicit agent-owned inter-S1 coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-harness session directories, main/builder channel separation, spec manifest and deterministic reload hooks. - Closure path: Builder changes trigger refresh of the main harness; this is capability adaptation, not conflict recognition and mutual inter-agent coordination. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate builder channel and session router do not establish S2 merely because two agents exist. - Evidence: web channels, harness update skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A custom generated harness could introduce its own S2 in code outside this reviewed standard arrangement.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-arrangement discretionary supervisory control over current priorities, resources or commitments of multiple operating S1 units was identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current agent execution status, multi-step progress, missing keys, safety limits and session lifecycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No manager uses a whole-system operational view to revise allocation or current commitments across agents. - Decision owner: User selects harness/model/policies; each model owns its own task; web server enforces channel/session management. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: updateplan, usage UI, model selector, RPC process lifecycle, static guard settings, session restart and status display. - Closure path: Plans and status feed back to the same task agent or user; no cross-unit supervisory current-control judgment returns to regulate the whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Local planning, visibility and process control do not establish a managerial S3 function by themselves. - Evidence: core, web, plan. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: An operator may stop/steer a session, but this alone is not a parent-governed whole-system S3 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary auditor challenges ordinary task-success claims and returns findings to a separate current-control owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect generated code, invalid specs, broken tools or misleading completion reports. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Validators and smoke/load checks mechanically test constraints but have no independent agent-owned audit judgment. - Decision owner: Forge&#39;s same task/build agent initiates checks; deterministic validators enforce configured formats, and the user may inspect output. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: forgevalidate, forgesmoke, view checker, type checks and generated-file edit detection. - Closure path: Validation errors return to the same builder agent for repair; no independent complementary audit/report-to-S3 route is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Testing an artifact, even in a separate subprocess, is ordinary build QA unless complementary independent audit ownership is established. - Evidence: Forge tools, update skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A developer can add independent review through custom code; not part of the first-party loaded mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Forge&#39;s model-based builder interprets prospective capability needs and develops changes to a target agent&#39;s future tools, skills, views, prompts and model settings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New domain requirements, tool gaps, user-identified failures and changing future tasks beyond the target agent&#39;s present tool repertoire. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a capability design, ask targeted clarifying questions, implement and iterate on candidate spec/code/skill changes for subsequent operation. - Decision owner: The Forge model agent, within user-defined scope and file/guard permissions; deterministic generation and smoke checks enforce its design. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scoped builderPrompt, harness-update, tool/view/skill-builder skills, forgegenerate, forgesmoke, reload and target-specific custom directories. - Closure path: User/future task distinction and existing harness inspection → model chooses a design → spec/code edits and validation → generator writes target package → web auto-reload or /reload exposes the new capability to later S1 turns. - Boundary reachability: The web server creates a dedicated Forge RPC builder for non-Forge harnesses and calls reloadHarness on settled modifications; Forge itself exposes build/update tools in normal model sessions. - External distinction: User-provided new workflow requirements and missing integrations, checked against target spec and tool inventory. - Future / prospective distinction: A new tool/skill/prompt is intended for subsequent tasks and new harness sessions, not simply to answer the current domain question. - Adaptation option generated: An agent-chosen modified harness.yaml, custom extension, skill and/or view, with validation and smoke-test plan. - Path back into current capability / S3: Generated assets and the main agent are reloaded; next user request can use new tools or knowledge. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Although the human requests a capability, the model chooses the implementation and artifacts; the generator does not determine what should be built. - Evidence: builder wiring, update skill, tools, README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Capability change is often user-initiated and may need approval/credentials; no autonomous long-horizon self-improvement mandate or guaranteed tested success is asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No binding adjudication of ultimate harness identity/policy disputes by an autonomous or evidenced parent S5 path was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Sandbox scope, protection of files, model/provider restrictions, acceptable tools and target identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Users select guards, models and targets in specs/settings; tool approval denies selected actions but no identity-level issue is escalated and ruled on through a first-party loop. - Decision owner: User/developer controls policy/configuration and can intervene per action; Forge may edit lower-level settings only within permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: harness.yaml guards, sandbox modes, protected paths, confirmation prompts, credentials and spec validation. - Closure path: Existing rules block or permit local actions; no legitimacy-governed S5 identity conversation and subsequent binding policy return is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A prompt/guard or proposed model change is not automatically ultimate-policy authority, and permission checks do not constitute S5. - Evidence: guards, sandbox, README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A human can rewrite the identity of a local generated harness, but this is not an established S5 governance channel.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9405fcfed091b78850008927c8c52e023dd1e4ba/assessments/pi/pi-forge.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9405fcfed091b78850008927c8c52e023dd1e4ba\">9405fcf &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-15T20:43:38+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Harvil1/codeAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CodeAgent (Harvil1)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-15\">2026-10-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Harvil1/codeAgent/tree/e48780be52949dfcf17338025f6beab48ca01428\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e48780be52949dfcf17338025f6beab48ca01428\">e48780b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Select coding actions from changing repository observations and execute model-directed file/shell/tool work until task completion or bounded stop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User coding requirements, file contents, tool success/failure, budgets, permission denials. - Decisive decision or feedback right: AIAgent&#39;s model selects tools and follow-up actions after receiving tool results; no human per-step selection required in allowed mode. - Decision owner: The packaged AIAgent/model-led loop; external provider performs inference but does not supply a separate task-owning agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent iteration budget and stop handling, permission gates, modeltools dispatch, sessions/context, tool adapters. - Closure path: runconversation → model request → dispatchtoolcalls → tool result/history → next runconversation iteration or final answer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: AIAgent owns the decision/observation/continuation loop and can operate locally through the shipped CLI. A provider API alone would not produce this tool-control loop. - Boundary reachability: The supported cli.main / runinteractive instantiates AIAgent; the model-tool loop and tools are shipped first-party. - Evidence: agent/init.py; cli.py; modeltools.py; agent/llmclient.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate specific duplicate assignment and unauthorized task-state interference among separately running child worker S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independently running workers may attempt to claim/operate the same kanban task and corrupt shared task ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic conditional task claim arbitrates ownership; coordinator reports contested-task failure to the caller, and binding refuses writes to another worker&#39;s task. - Decision owner: First-party constructed TaskStore/TeamCoordinator ownership guard; discretionary rescope after a rejection belongs to the parent model, not to the file lock. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cross-process lock, conditional status/owner update, CODEAGENTKANBANTASK binding, tasktools ownership enforcement. - Closure path: Parent selects a worker/task ID → coordinator claims task atomically → conflicting claim returns failure to parent as tool result → later parent assignment can change; admitted worker&#39;s task mutations are bounded by binding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is narrower than generic bus/routing: it directly regulates competing ownership of a shared task between independent worker processes with a returned failure signal. It does not prove general repository file-conflict resolution. - Boundary reachability: teamspawn with taskid and task updates are first-party registered team/task tools used by the shipped model loop. - Distinct S1 units: first-party separately running agent/team/worker.py child coding model loops, each possibly assigned a TaskStore task in one parent-managed team. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two workers or successive assignments targeting one mutable task can contend for a single task owner, causing duplicate task-state writes/commitments. - Attenuating coordination relation: TeamCoordinator invokes atomic TaskStore.claim before worker process startup; claim rejects a task owned by another worker and a bound worker cannot update a different task. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a rejected teamspawn/claim returns failure as a tool result to the parent model which can choose a different task/worker on a later loop; task store owner and status are visible to subsequent workers. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the conditional owner transaction directly rejects a concrete inter-worker duplicate-claim disturbance and returns the rejection to the choosing parent; this is narrow task-ownership collision attenuation, not general file/worktree conflict handling. - Evidence: agent/taskstore.py; agent/team/coordinator.py; agent/team/taskbinding.py; tools/teamtool.py; tools/tasktools.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate the live roster of child coding operations and their current task commitments at the main-agent team boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker availability, completion/failure, occupied task ownership, finite team capacity and conflicting active commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The parent model can inspect teammembers and task state and choose teamspawn, teamshutdown and task assignments during an active session. - Decision owner: The main AIAgent using shipped team/task tools; TeamCoordinator enforces member caps and process transitions but does not choose the plan. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Registry with member status/role, teammembers, task tools, spawn/shutdown, message bus, worker status updates. - Closure path: Live roster/task results enter the model&#39;s next turn → model chooses worker/task intervention → coordinator changes running processes / task owner → returned status informs subsequent turns. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Distinct from mere process manager: a model-operable whole-current team roster and intervention tools are wired into the same first-party feedback loop. This is a structural capability claim, not a measured autonomous scheduling benchmark. - Boundary reachability: Team tools are registered with the shipped agent&#39;s callable toolset, not a developer CI script or external scheduler. - Whole-system current view: teammembers returns status/role/PID of all first-party team workers, and the task store exposes assigned current work and ownership to the main agent. - Current-control decision scope: main model can choose which worker/task to spawn, inspect current membership, shut down running workers, and revise current task commitments; deterministic team size/depth ceilings enforce but do not choose those decisions. - Evidence: tools/teamtool.py; agent/team/coordinator.py; agent/team/worker.py; agent/init.py; tools/tasktools.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Provide sufficiently independent operational reality inspection with corrective return to the whole-current S3 controller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Child reports may be inaccurate, tool outputs incomplete, and operator state may drift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separately established organization-wide complementary audit judgment with corrective S3 return is demonstrated. - Decision owner: Child-output checks and optional aux-LLM review are supporting validators; a distinct S3 audit owner is not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child-claim filesystem/TaskStore check, appendwarning, optional handoffreviewenabled, trace logs. - Closure path: Claim mismatch warnings can reach parent context, but the verifier is scoped to a single delegation and does not own a complementary assessment of current system regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Cross-check of declared files/tasks is real yet narrowly local; source does not demonstrate an independent audit of S3 operations or an audit-based current-control corrective obligation. - Evidence: agent/team/hallucinationcheck.py; tools/delegatechild.py; agent/trace.py; config.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Monitor the external future environment and choose/provide prospective organizational adaptation to the current coding system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New external coding environments, future model/tool needs, shifts in operating strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party whole-system external/prospective adaptive decision right is demonstrated. - Decision owner: Developer/user controls extensions and learning enablement; heuristic skill evolution operates on past in-session trace patterns. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Disabled-by-default skilllearning observer/evolver, skill storage, memory/compaction, workflow and hooks. - Closure path: When enabled, repeated past tool patterns can become local skill files; the source does not close external-future sensing → strategic selection → changed operational capacity as an S4 function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An auto-generated habit from task traces is retrospective task improvement; the user-enabled feature by itself does not establish environment-facing prospective S4 governance. - Evidence: agent/skilllearning/turnobserver.py; agent/skilllearning/evolver.py; agent/skilllearning/observer.py; config.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Resolve legitimacy, ultimate identity and binding mission/policy choices of the harness organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting user goals, safety settings, credentials and authorization limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operators set higher-level objective/configuration; ordinary command approvals and system prompt constraints do not resolve organization-wide identity disputes. - Decision owner: Operator/developer owns settings, permissions and scope outside assessed autonomy; shipped code enforces rather than closes ultimate policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission subsystem, command/secret guards, config settings and model tool allowlists. - Closure path: Approvals or denials affect local tool calls; no supported first-party policy-identity escalation and authoritative returned decision loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Local tool-control policy and user acceptance are not themselves an operational S5 mission/policy closure. - Evidence: agent/permission.py; config.py; agent/init.py; tools/teamtool.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8b7f3f939013ab310a253b2b144d5f8e2654644a/assessments/codeagent-harvil1.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8b7f3f939013ab310a253b2b144d5f8e2654644a\">8b7f3f9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-18T14:18:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/uberblick-ai/ub-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ub-agents</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-18\">2026-11-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/uberblick-ai/ub-agents/tree/883bc1e4a241a573facaeb025489be59e7cb3428\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 883bc1e4a241a573facaeb025489be59e7cb3428\">883bc1e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Prepare work, implement changes and resolve bounded coding and integration decisions within the configured role. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Varying issue scope, repository contents, exact PR heads, check outcomes and reviewer findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select implementation actions, tests, and a task outcome to report. - Decision owner: First-party instructed model-backed role agent running via a configured CLI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Assignment context, worktrees, run lease, tool permissions and typed outcome transitions. - Closure path: Changed artifacts and outcome summaries persist to GitHub, and accepted observations enter later role runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The launcher dispatches and enforces policy. The assigned model selects substantive work and outcome. - Boundary reachability: Init generates the role set and the normal launcher invokes one configured runtime under the matching trigger. - Evidence: starter dispatch and handoffs, assignment and feedback, execution contract. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Deterministic claim arbitration regulates real competition between work units. A publishable agentic S2 mode was not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two launchers can attempt the same item or an issue and its linked PR branch simultaneously. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The earliest live claim wins and the losing launcher selects another eligible item by the shipped ordering rule. - Decision owner: Run coordination implements fixed claim arbitration. Project admin supplies ordering and timing configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Renewable claims, related work exclusion, GitHub rereads and operation-specific feedback. - Closure path: Losing contenders withdraw and replan. Related branch work waits until the conflicting live claim or cleanup clears. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The collision winner is fixed by GitHub comment order before a model starts. A model does not choose how to settle the conflict. - Evidence: same-item and linked-branch exclusion, priority and losing-claim feedback, election and withdrawal. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: A bounded absence of an A/C ownership arrangement. The shipped deterministic interference control is effective within its cooperative launcher contract.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Regulate current work commitments across the project queue when live work must stop, change priority or resume after an exception. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Blocked items, overlapping deadlines, active leases and assignments that need owner input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Approve continued work, hold or stop a chosen current commitment, or restore the selected role to eligibility. - Decision owner: Repository maintainer/operator with authority over project workflow labels and retry. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project-wide status and monitor, GitHub stop/trigger labels, lease renewal check and retry command. - Closure path: A stop label cancels the affected live run during renewal. A returned answer and retry or restored trigger determine subsequent execution under the same queue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model roles can report an exception while trusted GitHub state records the operator&#39;s current-control disposition. - Boundary reachability: Standard monitor/status views, eligible GitHub operator actions and the retry CLI are shipped. - Whole-system current view: Status exposes running, eligible, parked and blocked assignments with reason, priority, head, lease and responsible agent. - Current-control decision scope: The parent can interrupt or unblock a chosen commitment while keeping the rest of the project queue active. - Evidence: whole queue state, live stop semantics, operator response and retry. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: The scope is the current repository workflow. Per-item task choices and fixed queue order do not establish autonomous whole-queue discretion.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently challenge a candidate&#39;s claim to satisfy issue acceptance and supply the feedback that regulates revision or integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementation claims and summaries can miss a criterion, conceal a regression or refer to an old PR head. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose which checks and artifacts are sufficient, then render findings and a changes-requested or approved outcome. - Decision owner: Bundled reviewer role in the independently configured runtime mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Head provenance, separate role/worktree, runtime eligibility, review records and outcome transitions. - Closure path: Findings send the candidate back to the implementer or release the verified head for integration. Renewed heads are rechecked. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer performs discretionary judgment from the exact code, diff and candidate test evidence. - Boundary reachability: Init provides a supported separate reviewer runtime configuration backed by candidate/head provenance. - Claim being audited: The proposed head meets the issue criteria and has adequate check evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementer handoff, PR summary and automated check status. - Complementary access path: Reviewer opens source and exact candidate diff, consults candidate-specific check evidence and may run reachable check steps. - Independence boundary: A separate non-writing reviewer can require a CLI and model different from the known candidate producer. - Who acts on findings: A later implementer repairs the current candidate, or the integrator handles a head released by review. - Evidence: shipped reviewer checks and report, origin-runtime and head binding, findings enter later runs, feedback and correction. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Require the first-party supported independent reviewer mode for the stronger witness. The source records configured runtime identity, which is distinct from model weight independence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Plan a controlled change of the launcher fleet when an external release would alter compatibility and coordination. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Upcoming launcher builds can change coordination record formats or introduce configuration older launchers reject. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide to defer or execute a coordinated rollout, and choose which current commitments to park before restarting the upgraded fleet. - Decision owner: Project launcher operator responsible for all deployed launchers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Release-range checks, terminal upgrade/skip banner, drain and graceful stop, known install procedure and restart. - Closure path: The operator&#39;s rollout decision is applied to the fleet version and restart plan, changing later queue and lease behavior. The automatic route refuses an unsafe independent replacement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Metadata and version predicates control the automatic path. Timing, coordinated adoption and treatment of affected commitments belong to the operator. - Boundary reachability: Continuous launcher checks and reports incompatible release ranges, and the shipped operator workflow defines coordinated stop, upgrade and restart. - External distinction: Published distribution and release markers distinguish independent upgrades from coordination-format or configuration-breaking releases. - Future / prospective distinction: Current fleet runs an older format. A mixed-version fleet could lose shared live claim and retry semantics. - Adaptation option generated: Offer independent rollout where the release range allows it, or require coordinated drain/restart, leaving the current fleet running if the parent defers. - Path back into current capability / S3: The parent sequences the rollout and parks affected work, then the new launcher version resumes queue/lease control for future assignments. - Evidence: release alternatives and fleet return, supported v4 transition, bound release scan and automatic skip, release compatibility before upgrade. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: This positive parent path is specifically for launcher fleet compatibility. A periodic upgrade of an already-approved compatible component does not establish autonomous S4.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Set which classes of changes the entire project&#39;s agent workflow may integrate and which authorities retain ultimate disposition. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The starter policy may conflict with a repository&#39;s reserved change classes, release rules or designated legitimate maintainers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Approve or decline the proposed durable workflow-policy revision and set the resulting division of responsibility. - Decision owner: Project maintainers who approve the shared workflow policy into their default branch. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Init&#39;s policy tailoring issue, generated shared policy, maintainer-only merge rule and runtime reload. - Closure path: The proposed policy is routed to maintainers. Merged policy is refreshed and read before later assignment runs, affecting which changes agents may integrate and which must return to parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent authors can propose a repository-specific boundary. The maintainer decides policy, and each subsequent role is bound to the accepted version. - Boundary reachability: The installer offers the first policy-tailoring issue and the launcher refreshes the selected shared policy at runtime. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Choose which categories of change this installed workflow can integrate autonomously under the project mandate and which require legitimate parent authority. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The maintainers decide which continuing policy to merge or retain. - Return-to-operation path: The next role startup reloads merged instructions and uses the policy to merge only authorized change classes or send them to the named maintainer. - Evidence: shipped policy-tailoring intake, tailoring and reserved policy merge, continuing merge and human decision policy, reload returned policy. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: The witness is approval and runtime return of a policy revision affecting all future role runs. Routine approval of a particular PR is a lower-level decision.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7d093108c612178b30669da12518794ca326c481/assessments/ub/ub-agents.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7d093108c612178b30669da12518794ca326c481\">7d09310 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T17:33:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AmbientCrypto/ambient-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ambient CLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AmbientCrypto/ambient-cli/tree/46dfd8aada3eb73f4446b6b2e3c34c6b3c7a68b9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 46dfd8aada3eb73f4446b6b2e3c34c6b3c7a68b9\">46dfd8a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: make coding and repository investigation decisions from user goals, real workspace state and returned tools/results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: source unknowns, code errors, tool failures, user changes, model uncertainty, tests/diagnostics and delegated work outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select file/tool actions, revise edits, launch bounded children and respond to actual observations. - Decision owner: the main Ambient model-backed Agent, plus separately model-driven child agents within assigned read-only or writable mandates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compiled tool registry, permission/risk classifier, memory and context, model-controller lane, executor, session persistence, abort/budget limits and human confirmation. - Closure path: task/prompt + repository context → model-selected tool or delegation → first-party file/shell/tool execution → observed result/report → model revisions or completion. - Boundary reachability: normal ambient CLI wires first-party Agent with project tools and the advertised subagent tool; the child tool invokes actual new Agent(...).run(...) with scoped context and returns observed effects to the same parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing code edits and investigative responses needs model discretion; deterministic permissions and runtime cannot perform these decisions alone. - Evidence: Agent runtime, built-in child tool, tool registry. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: no live model-effectiveness claim; read-only/oracle and builder tools have distinct capabilities and approval paths.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: stabilize simultaneous independently acting worker S1 units competing for the operator&#39;s single question/approval resolution channel. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two builder children asking approval concurrently would each overwrite the same parent prompt resolver or strand a promise; independent child questions similarly collide at the user interaction endpoint. - Distinct S1 units: child Agent.run instances within the same runSubagents wave, each with separate task, model context, child ID, tool decisions and permission questions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous calls to the shared user-facing approval/question resolver can clobber another worker&#39;s outstanding request and prevent a correct answer reaching its author. - Attenuating coordination relation: a first-party per-wave approveChain queues approvals and askChain queues questions, while non-asking children continue operating in parallel. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each queued approval/question resolves against its original child tool action, allowing that child to execute or adapt/retry; the result is then included in its normal model/tool loop. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: source directly names and prevents a concrete mutual-interference failure among concurrent children over one scarce interactive decision slot, returning each decision to the correct continuing worker. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose how concurrent child approvals are serialized and resumed; shipped order is deterministic, authored by the developer, not renegotiated by another autonomous agent. - Decision owner: developer-provided first-party approval queue/coordination policy. The main model can choose which tasks to launch but does not autonomously decide the shared-resolver contention policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate child contexts and task IDs, promise chains, bounded parallel wave workers and role-limited permissions. - Closure path: concurrent child tool effects request approval → serialized resolver queue → original human decision delivered to each child → child executes/denies and continues with the observed result. - Boundary reachability: makeSubagentTool injects runSubagents into normal CLI tools, and its child Approver/AskPort are replaced with the shipped gated queues; this is not a test-only lock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents own their coding actions, but changing the S2 exclusion/ordering rule would require runtime modification or developer composition rather than the model&#39;s organizational discretion. - Evidence: runSubagents approval/question chains, CLI subagent instantiation, question/approval surface. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: attenuates question/approval-slot contention; does not claim general workspace conflict resolution or model negotiation among builders.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independently established whole-current resource/commitment manager with authority to revise a portfolio of S1 commitments beyond composing a current delegated coding wave. - Disturbance / variety regulated: background child progress, shared user approvals and per-role deadlines are managed for one requested task, not a durable independently accountable multi-mission portfolio. - Whole-system current view: the TUI sees child started/finished progress, and the main model receives child summaries; it does not receive a separate standing whole-current performance/commitment ledger spanning independent operations. - Current-control decision scope: the main model selects wave jobs/roles and integrates reports; the user can interrupt or request early wrap-up. Four-way concurrency and per-child time/turn caps are fixed runtime limits, not model-owned global resource bargaining. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate cross-unit current-control decision evidenced beyond initial delegation, ordinary task integration and human interruption. - Decision owner: model for operational decomposition; human and fixed runtime for approval/limits; no independently demonstrated S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runSubagents wave statuses, task records, completion summaries, fixed concurrency caps, soft/hard deadlines and parent cancellation. - Closure path: child task start → aggregate result into main coding task exists; independent current S1 commitment state → whole-system intervention → revised continuing child commitments is not wired as a separate control path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing tasks at one delegation point does not establish whole-current S3; task overview is not a discretionary organizational control loop without returned reallocations. - Evidence: child wave and caps, CLI subagent tool, runtime Agent. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a team-shaped task can be sizeable; a different broader organization integrating ongoing workspace management might support S3, but no such first-party control return is credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: provide an optional independent second-opinion read of actual repository artifacts, distinct from the main coding Agent&#39;s own narrative and ordinary post-edit checks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unobserved design/correctness mistakes or optimistic self-reporting after code changes may escape the author&#39;s tool/test account. - Claim being audited: that proposed or completed project code changes are sound for the requested design/task. - Ordinary reporting path: main model tools, builder output and current diagnostics/verify scripts return into its usual coding context. - Complementary access path: the built-in subagent tool exposes explicit oracle role for review; a separate model-backed child session with read-only file/search tools inspects source directly and reports independent findings. Auto routing selects reviewer-oriented fleet models (without guaranteeing distinct model identity). - Independence boundary: separate Agent instance/session, own prompt and context and enforced read-only tool set, rather than a rephrased main-agent summary; user-authored extra reviewer scripts or external platform evaluators are not credited. - Who acts on findings: the parent coding Agent, which receives and may act on the oracle&#39;s review summary; oracle cannot mutate files. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what shortcomings the directly observed code has, using model judgment in the separate review mandate. - Decision owner: the first-party instantiated oracle child model for semantic review, opt-in when the main Agent selects a review delegation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role-based tool registry, separate child session/history, RoutedRole=reviewer, summary cap, event/progress stream and parent result injection. - Closure path: existing code → separate read-only oracle&#39;s direct file inspection → reviewer-model assessment → returned child report → parent model can revise coding actions. - Boundary reachability: oracle is one of three advertised shipped subagent roles, is dispatched through actual new Agent(...).run, and read-only permissions are enforced in the child registry; no custom reviewer plugin is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separate model chooses semantic review findings; the deterministic read-only rule and output cap enforce isolation but do not make the audit judgment. - Evidence: role schema/registry, actual child reviewer model runner, reviewer role fleet route, system delegation instruction. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: optional, not mandatory; no live demonstration or empirical reviewer effectiveness. The oracle may resolve to the same underlying model family as its parent; independence here is context and direct read-only evidence, not guaranteed model-provider diversity.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: re-evaluate alternative external model capabilities from a changing live inference fleet and accumulated real-request performance before adopting a more suitable model for subsequent coding runs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: models become cold/unavailable, new candidates appear with differing tool/reasoning/context capability, and experience reveals persistent differences in request success and latency. - External distinction: the current Ambient live catalogue exposes per-model readiness and advertised capabilities, while local capability records persist observed provider outcomes across earlier requests. - Future / prospective distinction: a next run/phase may benefit from a different available model; a score ranks alternative candidates from current fleet plus historical success/latency instead of hard-coding one model identity. - Adaptation option generated: select the highest-scored ready, tool-capable candidate; role-specific planner/executor/reviewer/compactor selectors further specialize the next model choice. - Path back into current capability / S3: when requestedModel=auto or a role is routed, Agent.resolveModel applies the selected model and lane to the next model call, and subsequent outcomes update stored performance evidence for future decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine which external model-capability option replaces/serves the next coding model run based on reported and learned evidence. - Decision owner: developer-specified deterministic scoring and role-selection heuristics; no separately agent-owned S4 strategy actor is present. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ambient live catalogue, CapabilityStore, per-model sampled success/latency and tool-call evidence, model-resolution events, fallbacks and context budgeting. - Closure path: external fleet states + past request experience → bounded candidate scoring → selected role/default model applied for the next Agent request → outcomes recorded for later re-selection. - Boundary reachability: CLI injects makeCapabilityPort; runtime Agent calls resolveRequestedModel with its persisted stats and pickForRole on auto routed work, not merely a developer roadmap or bare library helper. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prospective option and adaptation return are implemented, but the decisive scoring policy is fixed TypeScript; an independent autonomous strategy selector/replanner would require additional composition. - Evidence: live-catalog scoring, future-model resolution and application, persisted learning port, run outcome capture. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: S4=C is restricted to adaptive model capability selection for later operations, not autonomous changes to the product&#39;s mission, tools, source or long-range business strategy. Role choice is heuristic, not validated as optimal, and historical outcomes need sufficient samples.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: preserve and legitimately revise a session-long ultimate coding objective when new evidence makes its north-star purpose obsolete or inconsistent with subordinate plans. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool-level plans or newly revealed constraints might conflict with the durable user-chosen mission; the agent must not silently move its own identity-level objective. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether to keep or replace the user-defined --goal//goal north star that explicitly outranks intermediate plans and governs every subsequent run in this session. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: legitimate human session owner in supported interactive mode. The model can propose a revision but cannot commit an identity-level change; no autonomous S5 mode claimed. - Return-to-operation path: model proposegoalupdate → interactive question including reason and proposed purpose → user confirms/edits or declines → only approval emits durable goal.set → pinned system-prompt goal and future turns follow the accepted identity. The explicit user /goal command also changes the purpose directly. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept, reject or rewrite the session mission itself, rather than approve one routine shell/edit action. - Decision owner: human operator/session owner; a model-generated proposal is subordinate and non-binding. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ctx.ask confirmation UI, reducer goal.set, session JSONL persistence, compaction goal anchor, next-turn system prompt and child goal inheritance. - Closure path: conflict with ultimate session purpose → agent proposal → legitimate human edit/confirmation → goal.set event → persistent next-turn prompt/children constrained by new user-chosen goal. - Boundary reachability: proposegoalupdate is registered as a first-party built-in tool and interactive TUI binds the approval to the live session with durable event return. In headless/no-user mode the proposal explicitly changes nothing, so S5=P is claimed only for supported interactive mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the human owner leaves the proposal without binding effect; the model cannot silently change the goal, so final identity decision belongs to parent. - Evidence: first-party goal proposal/approval, goal outranks plans, goal events in interactive session, goal restore. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is S5 at the coding session&#39;s purpose recursion only, not Ambient corporate/OSS identity governance; no session goal means the option remains dormant, and headless operation has no interactive parent return.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3463c6e3c2d7a4f339df2dd725fd2bfd2fb4f006/assessments/am/ambient-cli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3463c6e3c2d7a4f339df2dd725fd2bfd2fb4f006\">3463c6e &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
